{"raw": {"https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503235/payroll-and-benefits-coordinator": {"title": "Payroll and Benefits Coordinator", "description": "Payroll and Benefits Coordinator Apply now Work type: Permanent Full Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: Business and Financial Affairs Position Title Payroll and Benefits Coordinator About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department Human Resources supports Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. About the Position The Payroll and Benefits Coordinator performs technical payroll work supporting accurate and timely university payroll processing. The position enters, audits, reconciles, and corrects payroll and employee data; administers payroll deductions and benefit-related transactions; researches discrepancies across interconnected systems; and provides clear guidance to employees and departments. The position applies established laws, rules, collective bargaining agreements, policies, and procedures to varied payroll situations and coordinates complex issues with payroll, benefits, HR, finance, agencies, and service providers. Duties Include: Payroll Processing and Technical Support Perform assigned components of the university's regular payroll cycle for employees across multiple employee groups and bargaining units, following established schedules, procedures, contracts, and regulatory requirements. Enter, retrieve, audit, reconcile, and validate employee, timekeeping, leave, deduction, and payroll data in HR, payroll, financial, document-management, and external agency systems. Review source documents, calculations, reports, system messages, and rejected transactions; identify errors, determine appropriate corrections within established authority, and coordinate complex adjustments with the appropriate subject-matter expert. Process and maintain voluntary and involuntary payroll deductions, including benefit and retirement deductions, union dues, garnishments, supplemental retirement changes, and other authorized withholdings. Research payroll discrepancies by tracing transactions across systems and pay periods; reconcile records and explain the effect of corrections to employees and departments. Maintain accurate payroll records and documentation; support recurring audits, reconciliations, reporting, records retention, and compliance activities. Provide backup and collaborative support for payroll processing functions to maintain continuity during deadlines and peak workload periods. Benefits and Retirement Support Process and validate benefit and retirement enrollment, eligibility, coverage-change, qualifying-life-event, and deduction transactions that affect payroll. Reconcile internal payroll and HR records with benefit, retirement, and provider systems; research and resolve eligibility, enrollment, coverage, and deduction discrepancies. Explain established eligibility requirements, deadlines, documentation, payroll impacts, and available next steps to employees; refer plan interpretation or advisory questions to the appropriate benefits or retirement specialist. Support new employee enrollment, open enrollment, leave transfers, retirement consultation follow-up, pre- and post-payroll benefit processing, and recurring eligibility reports. Maintain accurate and confidential records and apply established requirements, including applicable PEBB, retirement, COBRA, HIPAA, and ACA procedures Employee Service, Outreach, and Cross-Functional Support Provide timely, accurate, and approachable assistance to employees and departments by phone, email, virtual meeting, and in person. Explain payroll, benefits, and retirement processes in clear language and connect employees with appropriate resources or subject-matter experts when needed. Support employee outreach through orientations, presentations, open enrollment activities, informational materials, and other education efforts. Develop and deliver informative training sessions and presentations to campus groups on key topics such as payroll processes, benefit enrollment, timekeeping and employee self-service tools Provide cross-functional support and perform related duties as assigned, including participation in special projects, process improvements, and compliance-related tasks to support the overall efficiency and effectiveness of the HR department Participate in system testing, process documentation, cross-training, special projects, compliance work, and continuous improvement activities supporting payroll and HR operations. Required Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree in Human Resources, Business Administration, Accounting or related field OR equivalent education/work experience 1-3 years of experience in payroll, benefits administration, or a related HR support role Familiarity with payroll and HRIS systems Excellent attention to detail and organizational skills Strong interpersonal and communication skills Ability to handle confidential information with integrity and discretion Preferred Qualifications Experience working in a fast-paced organization Experience working in higher education or public sector Basic understanding of accounting principles related to payroll Strong knowledge of federal and state payroll and benefits regulations Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary $4,652 - $6,257/month depending on qualifications and experience Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Classified Positions Bargaining Unit This position is part of bargaining unit A and is covered by the collective bargaining agreement with Washington Federation of State Employees. Application Instructions Application materials should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Closing Date Notes Position closes 11/02/2026 at 11:55 pm. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503235 Advertised: Oct 19 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Nov 02 2026 23:55 Pacific Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503247/director-of-development": {"title": "Director of Development", "description": "Director of Development Apply now Work type: Permanent Full Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: University Advancement Position Title Director of Development About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department University Advancement supports Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. All are encouraged to apply, including women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. About the Position Serving as a lead development officer, this position is charged with creating and implementing a comprehensive development program to engage and solicit alumni, parents, friends, community members, business, and civic leaders to achieve the fundraising goals for Western. The Director of Development is personally responsible for soliciting and closing major gifts of $25,000 or more. Furthering Western\u2019s mission requires strategy, creativity, and innovation, which are cultivated by an inclusive, diverse environment that respects and appreciates differences. Western is seeking professionals for these positions who will contribute to our mission of enhancing, increasing, and supporting a diverse and inclusive environment. This is a full-time position reporting to the Senior Director of Development, Major Gifts, in University Advancement. Annual success metrics are determined from both the activities associated with fundraising and the actual donation results. The Director of Development is responsible for the discovery, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship of Western donors. In this ever-changing profession, multiple channels are utilized to connect with and cultivate donors, including face-to-face, phone and video, with a key success metric focused on substantial contacts. This position requires travel in and out of state with a heavy focus on the Puget Sound area. Position Duties and Responsibilities Fundraising (70%) - Focus on strategically identifying, engaging, qualifying, cultivating prospects and then soliciting major gifts of $25,000 or more. Travel regionally and nationally to connect personally with constituents. Complete 70-125 substantial contacts and 18-24 major gift solicitations per year. Prepare written proposals, background reports, and other development materials for use during donor cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship activities. One Team, Our Team. Work collaboratively with members of the Annual Giving, Corporations and Foundation Relations, Donor Relations, Alumni Engagement, Communications, and Special Events teams in order to further the fundraising goals for the University. Design and implement a comprehensive development plan to increase philanthropic support for funding priorities established by the University and Academic Partners. Develop effective fundraising strategies in coordination and collaboration with the Senior Director of Development and members of the University Advancement team. Follow up with donors and prospects in a timely manner. Cultivate relationships with diverse communities and individuals that have not otherwise been approached. Steward donors by consistently and thoughtfully reporting on impact in creative and meaningful ways. Prospect Management (20%) - In collaboration with the Director of Prospect Research, build, develop, and manage a portfolio of 100-150 major and principal gift prospects. Execute timely contact reports. Consistently record and track all activity using the University's prospect management and tracking system. Prioritize reporting activities and recording asks in the database. Follow up with donors and prospects in a timely manner. Leadership, Philanthropic Strategy, and Planning (10%) - Collaborate with staff who provide support to college and department development efforts. Collaborate with other unit-based gift officers, the Corporate Relations and Foundations team and Director of Planned Giving to enrich the fundraising team, build cross-unit partnerships, and bring in multi-disciplinary gifts. Take time to continue to learn the fundraising profession, benchmark, and make professional connections. Utilize current engagement opportunities presented by the Alumni Engagement team, and in collaboration, create new engagement opportunities for constituents. Participate in and collaborate with the Alumni Association, Foundation, and unit-based Advancement Boards. Required Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree 3 years of experience in frontline fundraising or related field. Experience working with or for a non-profit Strong interpersonal skills and a customer service orientation. Poised, professional manner with the ability to express oneself clearly and empathetically in interactions with others including written and in-person. Demonstrated proficiency in using technology \u2013 Microsoft Office suite, Teams and Zoom. Ability to travel (including a valid driver\u2019s license) at least once a week within Washington State, and outside the state for overnight travel. Ability to work occasional nights and weekends. Preferred Qualifications Experience working on a campus in the higher education environment Experience with a Customer Relationship Management System (CRM) Proven success in face-to-face asks and the closing of gifts valued at $25,000 and above Experience working on a large-scale, organized fundraising campaign Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Hiring range is $90,714 - $104,323/year depending on qualifications and experience. Through longevity position tops out at $117,928/year. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Administrative Professional Position . Application Instructions A cover letter and resume are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Please include the names and contact information of three professional references. References will only be contacted if necessary, with notice provided beforehand. Closing Date Notes For priority consideration, please apply by November 9, 2026; position is open until filled. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503247 Advertised: Oct 19 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503248/director-of-gift-and-endowment-administration": {"title": "Director of Gift and Endowment Administration", "description": "Director of Gift and Endowment Administration Apply now Work type: Permanent Full Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: University Advancement Position Title Director of Gift and Endowment Administration About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department University Advancement supports Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. All are encouraged to apply, including women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. About the Position The Director of Gift and Endowment Administration is responsible for the comprehensive administration of charitable gifts and endowments in support of Western Washington University. Reporting to the Assistant Vice President for Advancement Services, this position serves as the institutional subject matter expert on donor intent, gift compliance, along with gift and endowment administration. The Director ensures all gifts and endowments are administered in accordance with donor agreements, institutional policies, and applicable state laws and regulations. This role partners closely with key units within the university: \u2022 Partners with Finance to align donor intent with financial management, while maintaining primary responsibility for gift compliance, agreement management, and advancement data integrity. \u2022 Partners with the Director of Donor Relations to assure all donors are stewarded appropriately. \u2022 Partners with frontline fundraisers and campus administration to assure donor intent is in compliance with state law and will be executed by the university. The Director plays a key leadership role in advancing operational excellence, mitigating institutional risk, and supporting a donor-centered culture aligned with the University\u2019s mission. Position Duties and Responsibilities Gift Administration Leadership (30%) - Provide leadership and oversight of all gift administration operations, ensuring accurate, timely, and compliant processing of charitable contributions and commitments. Direct the work of the gift administration team responsible for gift processing, receipting, document management, and data integrity in the CRM. Ensure donor intent is accurately recorded, interpreted, and reflected across systems and processes. Oversee implementation and continuous improvement of policies, procedures, and workflows that support efficient and compliant gift administration. Ensure timely and accurate generation and distribution of donor acknowledgements, receipts, and related communications. Maintain documentation of all gift administration policies and procedures to ensure compliance with federal and state regulations, IRS requirements, CASE standards, institutional policies, and campaign counting guidelines. Oversee coordination of gift-related transactions, including matching gifts, pledge tracking, fundraising-related payment mechanisms, and gift administration processes supporting comprehensive fundraising campaigns and targeted fundraising initiatives. Partner with Finance to support reconciliation processes, ensuring alignment between Advancement records and financial systems. Supervise professional staff and support a high-performing, service-oriented team culture. Donor Agreement & Endowment Management (35%) - In consultation with the CEO of the Foundation for WWU & Alumni, serve as the Foundation's authority on donor agreements, endowment administration, and interpretation of donor intent. Develop and implement policies and procedures for the creation, review, approval, and maintenance of donor agreements, including endowments, pledges, current-use gifts, and planned gifts. Review and authorize donor agreements for alignment with institutional standards, donor intent, applicable state and federal laws and regulations and compliance requirements prior to execution. Manage systems and tools used for agreement generation, tracking, and storage (e.g., Givzey, OnBase or similar platforms). Partner with development officers, university leadership, academic units, Finance, and donors to ensure agreements are clear, accurate, aligned with institutional priorities, and developed in consultation with University stakeholders to support successful implementation of donor intent; seek legal counsel as appropriate. Provide guidance and interpretation of existing donor and endowment agreements, including clarification of purpose, restrictions, allowable uses, and implementation requirements in partnership with Finance and campus stakeholders. Collaborate with Finance and other campus partners to support the implementation and monitoring of endowment spending policies, fund utilization, and compliance with donor restrictions and institutional policies. Ensure proper documentation, retention, and distribution of fully executed agreements. Collaborate with Donor Relations to support endowment reporting and donor stewardship communications. Maintain proactive communication with internal stakeholders to support effective use and understanding of donor-restricted and endowment funds. Compliance, Policy & Cross-Campus Coordination (25%) - Lead development and implementation of policies related to gift compliance, donor intent, and endowment administration. Serve as the primary Advancement resource for donor-related compliance matters, coordinating with Finance, Donor Relations, University legal counsel, Enrollment Management and academic partners as appropriate. Identify, assess, and mitigate risks related to donor restrictions, fund usage, and complex gift opportunities; provide guidance and analysis to Advancement leadership, the Foundation CEO, and other institutional decision-makers to inform gift acceptance review and approval processes. Review and authorize requests for new endowment, scholarship, unrestricted and restricted funds to ensure alignment with donor intent and institutional standards. Partner with Finance on the setting up of funds, distributions, and financial processes to ensure accurate implementation of donor intent. In partnership with Finance, collaborate with campus partners (e.g., Financial Aid, colleges, academic departments) to ensure a shared understanding of donor intent, endowment purpose, eligibility criteria, and fund restrictions, supporting the compliance with donor agreements and institutional policies. Develop and deliver training, guidance, and communication to Advancement and campus stakeholders regarding gift policies, endowment administration, and compliance requirements. Establish and maintain strong cross-functional relationships to support coordinated and compliant gift lifecycle management. Strategic Leadership & Other Duties (10%) - Serve as a strategic advisor to the CEO of the Foundation and University Advancement leadership on complex gifts, compliance risks, and endowment strategy. Provide expertise and guidance to University Advancement and campus stakeholders regarding emerging legal, regulatory, and policy issues that may affect donor agreements, fund administration, scholarship criteria, donor intent, and institutional compliance. Provide consultation and guidance to University Advancement leadership regarding vendor, service, and business agreements that support fundraising and advancement operations, identifying potential compliance, operational, and donor-related considerations and coordinating with University procurement and legal counsel as appropriate. Identify and implement process improvements to enhance efficiency, reduce risk, and improve the donor experience. Monitor emerging trends, regulations, and best practices in gift and endowment administration and recommend enhancements as appropriate. Contribute to division-wide initiatives and special projects. Perform other duties as assigned. Required Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree in Business, Finance, Accounting, or related field, or equivalent combination of education and experience. Minimum of five (5) years of progressively responsible experience in gift administration, endowment management, advancement services, or a related field. Strong analytical, organizational, and problem-solving skills with exceptional attention to detail. Excellent written and verbal communication skills with the ability to translate complex concepts for diverse audiences. Demonstrated commitment to customer service and collaborative partnership across functions. Experience supervising staff and leading operational functions. Commitment to advancing diversity, equity, and inclusion and supporting the University\u2019s mission, vision, and values. Preferred Qualifications Experience in higher education or a complex nonprofit fundraising environment. Demonstrated knowledge of donor intent compliance, IRS regulations, CASE standards, and nonprofit gift administration best practices. Experience with a CRM system (e.g., Ellucian Advance, Blackbaud Raiser\u2019s Edge, Salesforce). Working knowledge of accounting principles and financial reporting concepts. Experience with donor agreement development and negotiation. Experience reviewing, interpreting, drafting, or administering donor, legal, financial, or business agreements, including familiarity with compliance considerations related to charitable giving. Advanced degree, paralegal certification, Juris Doctor (JD), or equivalent education and experience in a related field. Demonstrated ability to lead cross-functional initiatives and influence stakeholders. Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Hiring range is $100,013 - $115,014/year depending on qualifications and experience. Through longevity position tops out at $130,016/year. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Administrative Professional Position . Application Instructions A cover letter and resume are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Please include the names and contact information of three professional references. References will only be contacted if necessary, with notice provided beforehand. Closing Date Notes For priority consideration, please apply by November 9, 2026; position is open until filled. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503248 Advertised: Oct 19 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503265/assistantassociate-professor-of-clinical-mental-health-counseling": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Clinical Mental Health Counseling", "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Clinical Mental Health Counseling Apply now Work type: TT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Humanities & Social Science Position Title Assistant/Associate Professor of Clinical Mental Health Counseling About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 consecutive years by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of Humanities and Social Sciences and the Department of Psychology support Western's mission, which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. All are encouraged to apply, including women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. The Department of Psychology has 33 permanent full-time faculty. In addition to our undergraduate B.S. in psychology major, the department offers an undergraduate degree in behavioral neuroscience and a master\u2019s degree in Experimental Psychology. We are also home to the internationally recognized Center for Cross-Cultural Research. The Counseling Program, with Clinical Mental Health Counseling and School Counseling specialty areas, has been accredited by CACREP since 1993. Both are fulltime, two-year cohort-based programs which enjoy considerable institutional support. The mission of the Counseling Programs is to prepare knowledgeable, skilled, culturally sensitive, and ethical professional counselors who meet the relevant licensing or credentialing standards for practice in mental health and educational settings in the State of Washington. We believe this is best accomplished in small rigorous programs in which students have substantive opportunities to acquire evidence-based knowledge and develop their professional and interpersonal skills under the supervision of faculty who are committed to training exceptional counselors. About the Position The Western Washington University Department of Psychology invites applications for appointment to a 9- month tenure-track position at the level of Assistant or Associate Professor in Counselor Education with a specialization in Clinical Mental Health Counseling, beginning September 16, 2027. The successful candidate will be expected to supervise practicum students in our community-serving onsite training clinic as well as internship students in the community, teach core counseling courses in their area of specialty, maintain a program of research, publish in peer-reviewed journals; provide Counseling Program service and leadership, and engage in service to the Department and University commensurate with experience. We are actively seeking candidates who show strong and consistent dedication to justice, equity, and inclusion throughout their career as a counselor educator. Required Qualifications Academic Experience: (a) PhD degree at time of application from a CACREP accredited counselor education program (b) minimum three years in a tenure-track/tenured position as a core faculty member in a CACREP accredited program OR minimum three years\u2019 experience in a leadership role of a CACREP program with current tenure-track appointment, (c) two semesters or three quarters teaching practicum, (d) two semesters or three quarters providing direct individual clinical supervision. Licensure: Currently fully licensed as a mental health counselor. The offer will be contingent on acquiring a Washington LMHC credential within 12 months of the hiring date. Scholarship: Evidence of an established and ongoing program of research, including planned, in progress and completed work that advances this program. At least one peer-reviewed journal article, accepted for publication in a quality outlet for counseling research, in which all aspects of the work were completed since the applicant completed their terminal degree. Evidence of additional scholarly accomplishment commensurate with experience. A strong and consistent dedication to social justice, equity and inclusion in counseling practice and counselor education, evidenced through teaching and supervision pedagogy commensurate with experience. For appointment at the Associate Professor level (tenure track), demonstrated significant research and evidence of successful teaching commensurate with standards for promotion to Associate Professor in the Department, College, and University. Preferred Qualifications Tenured at current institution Three years\u2019 experience as a CACREP core faculty member. Evidence of scholarship in the areas of equity, inclusion, and diversity. Leadership experience demonstrated through one or more of the following: (a) previous service as CACREP program director, (b) other program leadership, for example, internship coordinator, admissions coordinator, CACREP liaison/coordinator; (c) chaired college or university committee commensurate with experience. Professional identity and engagement. Presentations at ACA conferences, publication in counseling-related journals, service on counseling journal editorial boards, national service to the counseling profession, commensurate with experience. Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Commensurate with experience and qualifications. Anticipated starting salary range for assistant professor appointment, tenure-track is $72,000-$75,000 with placement within the position\u2019s salary range being based on qualifications and professional experience. The entire salary range for the assistant professor position is $72,000 - $84,500 with the upper end of the salary range typically being achieved through collectively bargained salary adjustments. For Associate Professor appointment, tenure track, anticipated starting range is $82,000-$84,000 with placement within the position\u2019s salary range being based on qualifications and professional experience. The entire salary range for the associate professor position is $82,000 - $97,500 with the upper end of the salary range typically being achieved through collectively bargained salary adjustments. Relocation assistance may be available per University guidelines and dependent on available budget. Salary and Start-up funds are to be determined upon being offered the position. In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the entire salary ranges provided above reflect the anticipated full salary range in the position at the given rank, with progression in ranks occurring as outlined in the University\u2019s tenure and promotion process, per the collective bargaining agreement. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions Please submit your application to Professor Brent Mallinckrodt and the search committee through Western\u2019s PageUp system. Please include a cover letter that addresses your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. The letter should summarize the ways in which your strengths are consistent with the values of the department and the program priorities outlined for this position, including your commitment to working toward equity and inclusion, and your preferred graduate teaching specialization. The application should also include: A curriculum vitae. An autobiographical statement limited to a total of 1500 words that addresses these three elements: examples of your previous experience building community, promoting social justice, and/or expanding educational opportunity for graduate students from underrepresented groups description of the values and pedagogical principles that guide your graduate teaching and clinical supervision, including how you have responded to feedback, outline of the program of research you hope to pursue in our department of psychology. Names and contact information for three references. Please do not upload reference letters. We will only contact references of applicants who are selected as semi-finalists. Semi-finalists will be contacted to confirm they wish to proceed to the next stage of selection. Reference letters will be required at the semi-finalist stage and these will be requested of the provided references via the PageUp application system. Semi-finalist applicants will also be asked to provide a teaching portfolio including sample syllabi from a practicum course and one other graduate offering. Western Washington University sponsors employment-based work visas on a case-by-case basis. Eligibility for visa sponsorship depends on the position, the candidate\u2019s qualifications, and applicable immigration requirements. To determine whether visa sponsorship may be available for this position, please contact Kaylan Rocamora at rocamok@wwu.edu . For more information about Western Washington University, see the Psychology Department's web page . Closing Date Notes Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026; position is open until filled Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503265 Advertised: Oct 19 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503267/event-attendantusher": {"title": "EVENT ATTENDANT/USHER", "description": "EVENT ATTENDANT/USHER Apply now Work type: Temporary Part Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: Business and Financial Affairs Position Title Event Attendant/Usher About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The Box Office supports Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. All are encouraged to apply, including women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. About the Position Temporary Event Operations Assistant provides customer service and operational support for campus-wide events, including Athletic events, Performing Arts events, Cultural events and rentals. Temporary Event Operations Assistant also assists with larger events, festivals and tournaments held on Western's campus. The primary duties of this position are to provide general logistical support and assistance for campus events. This position works closely with other Event Operations Assistants for set-up and tear-down, assists with patron safety, event promotions, addressing the public, and provides general event operational needs. Hours are typically during event times - evenings and weekends with occasional daytime hours, depending on event schedules. Shifts usually begin 1.5 hours prior to event start time and end 1-2 hours after the event has started. Temporary Event Operations Staff represent Western to our patrons and campus visitors. Excellent customer service is essential to perform job functions. This position works a maximum of 1050 hours or 1 year (whichever comes first). Required Qualifications Attention to detail Excellent communication skills Ability to deescalate situations Ability to be flexible with hours Ability to work evenings, weekends and while Western is not in session. Customer service oriented Demonstrated ability to work independently and on a team while managing multiple projects and priorities Ability to handle private and personal information Preferred Qualifications Cash Handling experience Customer Service experience Technological troubleshooting experience Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary $19.26 - $24.97/hour depending on qualifications and experience Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Classified Positions This position is not eligible for benefits. Bargaining Unit Temporary This position is covered by a Washington Federation of State Employees bargaining union. New employees are eligible for membership under the current collective bargaining agreement at 350 hours of employment. Application Instructions Application materials should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Closing Date Notes Open until filled Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503267 Advertised: Oct 19 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503223/art-history-nontenure-track-faculty-vacancy-pool": {"title": "Art History - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool", "description": "Art History - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool Apply now Work type: NTT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Fine & Performing Arts Position Title Art History - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of Fine and Performing Arts and the Art & Art History Department support Western's mission, which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. The Department of Art and Art History provides a transformational education that encourages curiosity and facilitates critical inquiry, collaboration, creative problem solving, and community engagement. Through rigorous scholarship, studio practice, global visual arts studies, and experiences beyond the classroom, students gain knowledge and skills that prepare them for successful careers and global citizenship. The three interrelated areas of Art Studio, Art History, and Art Education affirm the liberal arts tradition of the university and inspire innovation, active learning and community leadership. About the Position The Art & Art History Department maintains a vacancy pool of qualified persons interested in temporary teaching positions. In anticipation of possible openings throughout the academic year and summer sessions, applications are accepted continuously for temporary non-tenure track positions on a part-time basis with occasional opportunities for temporary, full-time appointments. Positions may become available at any time and are typically filled on a quarterly basis. This pool is specifically for candidates interested in teaching art history courses. Position Responsibilities: Instruction in various Art History courses assigned by Department Chair. Required Qualifications MA/MFA (or equivalent experience) Demonstrated excellent communication skills and professionalism Commitment to working with diverse students and staff, including cultivating learning environments that are equitable and inclusive of students with diverse social identities and backgrounds Preferred Qualifications University level teaching experience Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Commensurate with experience and qualifications Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions A cover letter and curriculum vitae are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. You will be asked to provide contact information of three references. This vacancy pool is refreshed annually; please see applicable close date information below. Applicants who continue to remain interested in being considered for on-going opportunities should re-submit updated materials to the new pool, once posted. Required Supplemental Materials An unofficial transcript showing degree received or a copy of diploma showing degree received. Closing Date Notes Applications are accepted continuously for potential NTT faculty openings; please apply at your earliest convenience. Vacancy pool closes no later than August 31, 2027. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503223 Advertised: Oct 15 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Aug 31 2027 23:55 Pacific Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503272/chemistry-nontenure-track-vacancy-pool": {"title": "Chemistry Non-Tenure Track Vacancy Pool", "description": "Chemistry Non-Tenure Track Vacancy Pool Apply now Work type: NTT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Science & Engineering Position Title Chemistry Non-Tenure Track Vacancy Pool About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of Science and Engineering and the Chemistry Department support Western's mission, which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. All are encouraged to apply, including women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. The Chemistry Department offers a robust schedule of lecture and/or laboratory instruction, primarily in 100-level general chemistry lecture/laboratory courses, organic and biochemistry laboratories, or upper division lecture courses in analytical chemistry, organic chemistry, biochemistry, and/or physical chemistry. Please refer to the department website for detailed information about our programs. About the Position The Chemistry Department maintains a vacancy pool of qualified persons interested in temporary teaching positions. In anticipation of possible openings throughout the academic year and summer sessions, applications are accepted continuously for temporary non-tenure-track teaching positions on a part-time basis with occasional opportunities for temporary, full-time appointments. Positions may become available at any time and are typically filled on a quarterly basis. Position Responsibilities: The department, College and University value teaching, and we expect you to be an excellent teacher; to be current in your discipline; to engage students actively in their own learning including discussion, writing, and analysis; and to set high standards regarding course content. You are expected to provide students with feedback on their coursework and examinations, and to be available to students through regularly scheduled office hours in keeping with department policy. You must have your students evaluate your course(s) using the standard University evaluation forms, or others approved by the department, and you must make those evaluations available to the department chairs and faculty for annual review. Required Qualifications M.S. in chemistry or related field (e.g., biochemistry, chemical engineering). Experience and skills in working with diverse students and staff, including a commitment to cultivating learning. environments that are equitable and inclusive of students with diverse social identities and backgrounds. Demonstrated potential for successful teaching at the college level. Preferred Qualifications Ph.D. in chemistry or related field (e.g., biochemistry, chemical engineering). Demonstrated successful experience in teaching chemistry (lecture and/or laboratory) at the college level Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Commensurate with experience and qualifications Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions A cover letter and curriculum vitae are required and should address the areas of chemistry/biochemistry that you are qualified to teach and your teaching experience in these areas, your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications, and your availability: part-time only, full-time only, or either. You will be asked to provide contact information for three references. Required Supplemental Materials If available, evidence of teaching ability (i.e., student course evaluations or peer teaching evaluations). Closing Date Notes Applications are accepted continuously for potential NTT faculty openings; please apply at your earliest convenience. Vacancy pool closes no later than October 31, 2027. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503272 Advertised: Oct 15 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Oct 31 2027 23:55 Pacific Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503264/physics-poulsbo-nontenure-track-faculty-vacancy-pool": {"title": "Physics - Poulsbo - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool", "description": "Physics - Poulsbo - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool Apply now Work type: NTT Faculty Location: Poulsbo, WA Categories: College of Science & Engineering Position Title Physics - Poulsbo - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool About the University & Western on the Peninsulas Western Washington University (WWU), with over 14,500 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. Western's main campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, with academic centers on the Olympic and Kitsap Peninsulas to support degree attainment and regional growth in partnership with Olympic and Peninsula Colleges. WWU currently offers nine BA and BS degrees, including a Cybersecurity BS degree, through in-person and hybrid programs to meet the needs of the region's diverse student population. About the Department The College of Science and Engineering and the Department of Physics & Astronomy support Western's mission, which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. About the Position The Physics & Astronomy Department at Western Washington University is seeking applications for full- and part-time non-tenure-track (NTT) instructor appointments to teach physics courses, including Physics with Calculus I & II, in Poulsbo, Washington in support of the Department of Computer Science\u2019s Cybersecurity Program. Courses in the program in Poulsbo are offered in nontraditional formats, including extended class periods, afternoon or evenings, and video assisted formats. Appointments may be quarterly or for a 9-month period dependent on the opportunities. Positions may become available at any time. Required Qualifications Masters degree in Physics, Astronomy or related area by date of appointment. Evidence of effective teaching at the lower undergraduate level in physics courses. Demonstrated commitment to cultivating learning environments that are equitable and inclusive of students with diverse identities and backgrounds. Preferred Qualifications PhD in Physics, Astronomy or related area by date of appointment. Evidence of effective teaching in undergraduate physics lab courses. Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Commensurate with experience and qualifications Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions A cover letter and curriculum vitae are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. As part of the application, you will be asked to provide the names and contact information of three references. If reference letters are needed, the PageUp online application system will generate requests to your three references for upload of reference letters. This vacancy pool is refreshed annually; please see applicable close date information below. Applicants who continue to remain interested in being considered for on-going opportunities should re-submit updated materials to the new pool, once posted. Closing Date Notes Applications are accepted continuously for potential NTT faculty openings; please apply at your earliest convenience. Vacancy pool closes no later than September 30, 2027. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503264 Advertised: Oct 09 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Sep 30 2027 23:55 Pacific Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503249/chief-procurement-officer": {"title": "Chief Procurement Officer", "description": "Chief Procurement Officer Apply now Work type: Permanent Full Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: Business and Financial Affairs Position Title Chief Procurement Officer About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The Business Services Department supports Western\u2019s mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. All are encouraged to apply, including women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. Business Services helps the Western community navigate the policies, systems, and requirements that support purchasing, contracting, travel, and other essential university activities. The department is committed to providing practical guidance, responsible stewardship of university resources, and accessible, reliable service to campus partners. About the Position The Chief Procurement Officer provides strategic vision and operational leadership for the University\u2019s Procurement and Contracts Office, Travel Services, Asset Inventory, and P-Card Program and reports to the Assistant Vice President for Finance and Business Services. The position designs and maintains the organizational structure and staffing necessary to accomplish the unit\u2019s strategic goals and oversees approximately $100 million in annual institutional spending. The Chief Procurement Officer directs daily operations; oversees staff recruitment, training, supervision, and evaluation; analyzes workflows; establishes priorities, standards, and deadlines; and implements improvements that strengthen processes and customer service. The position also evaluates and resolves complex procurement and contracting matters, authorizes purchases, and approves contracts in accordance with the Washington State Procurement Code. LEADERSHIP/TEAMWORK \u2022 Cultivates a workplace environment that is equitable and inclusive for individuals from diverse social identities and backgrounds. \u2022 Motivates and inspires staff to perform excellent work. \u2022 Works with senior leadership and internal/external customers to lead implementation of strategic supply chain initiatives that provide value, increase efficiency, and manage operational costs. \u2022 Has Signing Authority up to $250,000 purchases and determines Independent Contractor Determinations \u2022 Works with internal/external stakeholders to develop, maintain, revise, and communicate supply chain strategy and tactics, overall policies, guiding principles, performance measures and targets, and procurement value and cost management objectives to align with strategic goals. \u2022 Develops and champions the strategic vision to achieve targeted outcomes for customer service, process efficiencies/effectiveness, and management of operational costs. \u2022 Promotes team building, trust, respect, and collaboration amongst staff. \u2022 Engages staff by communicating strategic goals and how it relates to their individual goals. \u2022 Establishes and maintains alliances with other universities, divisions, and outside organizations to identify dependencies and assess potential partnerships. \u2022 Supervises Procurement and Contracts Program Managers, P Card Program Manager and Software and Technology Contract Specialist Lead \u2022 Is responsible for overall management of P Card Program, managing spend and rebates to support the University's Mission \u2022 Represents the university and Business and Financial Affairs division through participation on committees and strategic initiatives. CONTRACT ADMINISTRATION \u2022 Oversees development and execution of all non-employment contracts, renewals, and reviews research grants when requested. \u2022 Ensures legislative procurement requirements are met for contract awards including procurement, leasing, competitive contracting, and operational services agreements. \u2022 Directs and assists contract owners and stakeholders on resolution of client issues, vendor or contractor grievances, and claims. \u2022 Evaluates proposed projects to measure the benefit to campus users and creates an effective implementation strategy. \u2022 Conducts major solicitations and contract negotiations; executes or approves purchase contracts on behalf of the university. Responsible for contract review, modification, and writing as necessary. \u2022 Develops Strategic Vendor Strategy managing spend in key commodities. PROPERTY MANAGEMENT \u2022 In conjunction with property management firms, accomplishes financial and maintenance objectives for property management at the university. \u2022 Contracts with lessors and tenants on lease negotiations. \u2022 Provides a significant role in property purchases and sales. POLICIES, LAWS, AND REGULATIONS \u2022 Analyzes Washington state laws, rules, and policies, and uses independent judgment to determine whether expenditures, activities and processes comply with laws, regulations, or standards. \u2022 Provides policy, procedure, or regulation-related information to internal/external clients, supervisors, and staff to facilitate achievement of strategic goals. \u2022 Establishes and implements university and department policies, goals, objectives, and procedures. \u2022 Monitors operations to ensure staff comply with administrative policies and procedures, safety rules, union contracts, and government regulations. OPERATIONS \u2022 Evaluates and resolves complex procurement matters and institutes necessary changes to streamline and improve the purchasing and contract development processes. \u2022 Develops strategic sourcing opportunities to increase effectiveness of eProcurement and other purchasing objectives. \u2022 Directs daily operations of business unit activities, analyzes workflow, establishes priorities, develops standards, maintains effective customer service, and sets deadlines. \u2022 Performs business process analysis to evaluate and analyze business operations to meet objectives. \u2022 Evaluates proposed projects to measure the benefit to campus and creates implementation plans. \u2022 Develops annual administrative operating budgets and forecasts for business units. \u2022 Manages business related risks while taking advantage of business opportunities. \u2022 Maintain a current understanding of the financial situation of assigned funds and adhere to the financial manager responsibilities as defined in university policy POL-U5320.03. Ensure that funds are spent, obligated, and/or committed in accordance with all university policies and procedures, standards, state and federal rules, and applicable grant and contract language, and within available budget. This may include developing spending plans and forecasts for managed funds, oversight of individuals with delegated spending authority, and additional training on financial principles. Oversee individuals who have been delegated as a Budget Authority to ensure that these individuals understand the policies and procedures and that their expenditures are within budget and compliant with university standards. COMMUNICATION \u2022 Embraces access, diversity, engagement, and inclusivity in decision-making processes. Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree or five years of progressively responsible senior level experience in business administration. Ten years of experience in procurement, supply chain management, or a related field. Demonstrated expert decision-making, analytical, communicative, and negotiation skills together with knowledge of business and industry practices in both the private and public sectors. Demonstrated commitment and ability to cultivate work environments that are equitable and inclusive for individuals from diverse social identities and backgrounds. Preferred Qualifications Master\u2019s degree. Demonstrated eProcurement knowledge. Certified Public Purchasing Officer (CPPO), Certified Professional Purchasing Manager (CPPM), or Certified Professional in Supply Management. Experience with Banner or similar ERP software Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Hiring range is $121,566 - $139,801 depending on qualifications and experience. Through longevity position tops out at $158,037/year. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Administrative Professional Position . Application Instructions A cover letter and resume are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Please include the names and contact information of three professional references. References will only be contacted if necessary, with notice provided beforehand. Closing Date Notes Application review begins October 27, 2026; position is open until filled Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503249 Advertised: Oct 06 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503176/university-instrument-center-laboratory-technician": {"title": "University Instrument Center Laboratory Technician", "description": "University Instrument Center Laboratory Technician Apply now Work type: Project Full Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: Academic Affairs Position Title University Instrument Center Laboratory Technician About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department Scientific Technical Services (SciTech or STS) is a department that supports research, scholarship and teaching across all colleges at WWU by providing access to advanced multi-user scientific instrumentation, machine and electronics shops, and equipment design, fabrication and repair services. SciTech operates two core facilities: The University Instrument Center, Western\u2019s primary multi-user equipment facility with chromatography, mass spectrometry, and spectroscopy instrumentation and the University Shops, with electronics, metalworking, woodworking, and design and repair services. About the Position The University Instrument Center (UIC) Lab Tech is an entry-level professional position within Scientific Technical Services (SciTech), a department that supports research, scholarship, and teaching across all colleges at Western Washington University through advanced multi-user scientific instrumentation, machine and electronics shops, and equipment design, fabrication, and repair services. Working under the guidance of the Director and other experienced SciTech staff, the UIC Lab Tech assists with the operation, maintenance, and user support of scientific instrumentation used in instructional and research activities. Responsibilities include training users on routine instrument operation, monitoring instrument use, assisting with analytical testing, supporting classroom and laboratory demonstrations, and helping maintain laboratory and instrumentation facilities. This position is designed to provide opportunities for professional growth and skill development in analytical instrumentation, laboratory operations, and research support. The successful candidate will demonstrate strong technical aptitude, attention to detail, a commitment to customer service, and a willingness to learn new instrumentation and analytical techniques. Prior expertise with all instrumentation is not expected, as training will be provided. The UIC Lab Tech does not have supervisory responsibilities and works collaboratively with faculty, staff, and students to support a safe, effective, and accessible laboratory environment. The State HR classification for this position is Instructional & Classroom Support Technician 3. Funding for this position is provided for an initial one-year term and may be renewed, subject to available funding and programmatic needs. Training and User Support Train faculty, staff, students, and external users in the safe and effective operation of scientific instrumentation. Assist users with instrument setup, operation, data collection, and routine data processing for instructional and research activities. Provide guidance on sample preparation, instrument capabilities, analytical workflows, and established best practices. Assist in troubleshooting routine instrument, software, and sample preparation issues and refer complex problems to senior staff or faculty as appropriate. Develop and maintain standard operating procedures, user guides, training materials, and other instructional resources. Support classroom, laboratory, and research activities through demonstrations and hands-on instruction. Foster a safe, collaborative learning environment that promotes user proficiency and confidence with scientific instrumentation. Operations Support Support the day-to-day operation of the UIC by maintaining instrumentation, laboratory spaces, and equipment in a safe, organized, and functional condition. Prepare reagents, calibration standards, quality control samples, and other materials for instructional and research activities. Assist with the procurement, inventory management, and organization of equipment, supplies, chemicals, and consumables. Handle, store, and dispose of chemical waste and hazardous materials in accordance with university policies and regulatory requirements. Maintain records related to instrument maintenance, laboratory operations, and safety activities. Develop and maintain technical expertise in analytical instrumentation, laboratory practices, and emerging technologies. Maintaining Instrumentation Perform routine maintenance, operational checks, calibrations, and minor troubleshooting to ensure reliable and safe instrument operation. Identify instrument performance issues and coordinate repairs with vendors, service providers, senior staff, or faculty as needed. Maintain service records and documentation related to instrument maintenance and performance. Provide basic instrumentation support, troubleshooting, and maintenance assistance to other academic units as requested. Coordinate equipment scheduling to ensure efficient and equitable access for instructional, research, and service activities. Monitor instrument availability and communicate maintenance schedules, outages, and operational updates to users. Compressed Gases Monitor inventory levels of compressed gases and related laboratory supplies used within the UIC. Coordinate the ordering, receipt, storage, and replacement of gas cylinders and associated materials to support uninterrupted operations. Ensure compressed gases are handled, stored, and documented in accordance with university safety policies and applicable regulations. Professional Development and Technical Growth Develop expertise in the operation, maintenance, and application of scientific instrumentation. Assume increasing levels of responsibility as technical knowledge and competencies are acquired. Build proficiency in chromatography (GC and LC), ICP, and microCT instrumentation. Expand expertise into mass spectrometry, advanced imaging, and other specialized analytical techniques as experience and proficiency increase. Participate in ongoing professional development to support the evolving needs of the UIC and its users. Other Duties as Assigned Perform other duties and special projects in support of the teaching, research, service, and operational missions of the UIC and SciTech. Contribute to departmental initiatives and emerging operational needs as assigned. This is a project-employment position ending no later than June 30, 2027. Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree in a scientific or technical discipline (e.g., chemistry, biochemistry, biology, physics, geology, environmental science, materials science, or engineering), or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Demonstrated commitment to providing high-quality customer service and technical support. Ability to work independently under general supervision while effectively managing multiple priorities and collaborating professionally with SciTech staff, faculty, university staff, students, and external users from diverse backgrounds and experience levels. Strong verbal, written, and interpersonal communication skills, along with demonstrated problem-solving, troubleshooting, and organizational abilities. Experience operating scientific instrumentation and performing laboratory procedures in instructional, research, industrial, or other technical laboratory environments. Experience preparing samples, reagents, solutions, and standards; collecting, organizing, and documenting scientific or technical data; and following established laboratory procedures and safety protocols. Ability to recognize and respond to shifting departmental priorities by appropriately reprioritizing responsibilities, including adapting to short-notice changes in planned work activities to meet operational and user support needs. Commitment to functioning as a collaborative and dependable team member in support of shared departmental goals, including the effective operation of the UIC and the success of its users. Proficiency with standard computer applications, including Microsoft Word, Excel, and PowerPoint. Preferred Qualifications Experience supporting laboratory instruction, scientific research, instrumentation, or related technical operations. Experience operating, maintaining, or troubleshooting analytical instrumentation in instructional, research, industrial, or shared-use laboratory environments. Demonstrated experience with one or more of the following instrument categories: Chromatography systems, including GC, HPLC, UHPLC, or related techniques. Mass spectrometry instrumentation, including GC-MS, LC-MS, MALDI-MS, or related systems. Optical microscopy instrumentation, including brightfield, fluorescence, confocal, or Raman microscopy. Electron microscopy instrumentation, including SEM, EDS, or related techniques. Spectroscopic instrumentation, including UV-Vis, FTIR, Raman, fluorescence, atomic absorption, ICP-OES, ICP-MS, or related methods. Experience with instrument control software, scientific data analysis, and interpretation of analytical results. Experience developing or following laboratory protocols, standard operating procedures (SOPs), user guides, or other technical documentation. Experience providing technical instruction, user training, mentoring, or laboratory support in educational, research, or shared-use laboratory settings. Demonstrated ability to learn new instrumentation, software platforms, and analytical techniques and apply those skills in support of teaching, research, and service activities. Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary $4,429 - $5,952/month depending on qualifications and experience Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Classified Positions Bargaining Unit This position is part of bargaining unit PT and is covered by the collective bargaining agreement with Public School Employees of WWU. Application Instructions Application materials should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Closing Date Notes Application review begins October 23, 2026; position is open until filled. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503176 Advertised: Oct 05 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503257/honors-college-nontenure-track-faculty-vacancy-pool": {"title": "Honors College - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool", "description": "Honors College - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool Apply now Work type: NTT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: Academic Affairs Position Title Honors College - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The Honors College supports Western's mission, which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. All are encouraged to apply, including women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. About the Position The Honors College maintains a vacancy pool of qualified persons interested in temporary teaching positions to instruct in a variety of general education classes in the humanities, social sciences, and natural sciences. Applications are accepted at any time, and positions may become available at any time during the academic year. Positions are normally for one course during the academic year. Classes are small, limiting at 25, maximum. The Honors College is designed for students seeking an enhanced educational experience, and though students come from a broad variety of major areas, more are in the natural sciences than any other group of fields. Students expect to be actively involved in their classes and to write, or to be involved in experimental work and research as a matter of course in their Honors classes. Faculty are expected to introduce students to the widest possible variety of approaches to subject matter typical of their fields. Position Responsibilities: The college values and expects genuine excellence in teaching, and instructors are expected to be current in their disciplines, to encourage student participation in their educations, to demand writing or other work appropriate to the discipline, to maintain rigorous academic standards, to provide extensive commentary or other feedback on student work in a timely manner, and to be available to students through regularly scheduled office hours. Instructors must have their classes evaluated using the standard university student evaluation forms and to make those reviews available to the program. Required Qualifications Ph.D. or other terminal degree in the faculty member\u2019s discipline Demonstrated successful teaching experience at the college or university level Demonstrated commitment to cultivating learning environments that are equitable and inclusive of students with diverse social identities and backgrounds Preferred Qualifications Experience in interdisciplinary teaching Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Commensurate with experience and qualifications Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions A cover letter and curriculum vitae are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. You will be asked to provide contact information of three references. Closing Date Notes Applications are accepted continuously for potential NTT faculty openings; please apply at your earliest convenience. Vacancy pool closes no later than October 31, 2027. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503257 Advertised: Oct 05 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Oct 31 2027 23:55 Pacific Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503237/undergraduate-internship-manager-college-of-business-and-economics": {"title": "Undergraduate Internship Manager, College of Business and Economics", "description": "Undergraduate Internship Manager, College of Business and Economics Apply now Work type: Permanent Part Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Business and Economics Position Title Undergraduate Internship Manager, College of Business and Economics About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of Business and Economics supports Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. All are encouraged to apply including women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. We are a student-focused school of business and economics engaged in scholarly and professional activities that contribute to the well-being of society. We believe business education should empower individuals to lead with purpose, think critically, and create meaningful change in their communities and beyond. About the Position The primary purpose of this position is to cultivate internship opportunities and expand undergraduate student internship placement in the College of Business and Economics (CBE). Through employer outreach, partnership development, and coordination with academic programs, this position helps students gain practical experience, develop professional skills, and connect academic learning with career opportunities. Working collaboratively with employers, alumni, faculty, university partners, and the business community, the position supports student success, workforce readiness, and internship participation in alignment with the CBE Strategic Plan and AACSB accreditation expectations. The position works closely with the Undergraduate Career Services Manager to ensure students have access to and are professionally prepared to take on internship opportunities that support their academic programs, career interests, and professional goals. This is an in-person, on-campus position located at Western Washington University in Bellingham, Washington. This a permanent, part-time position (10-month cyclic). Position Duties and Responsibilities 50% - Develop internship pipelines and opportunities for CBE students. Building and maintaining relationships with employers, alumni, advisory board members, professional associations, economic development organizations, and community partners. Identifying, cultivating, and expanding internship opportunities aligned with CBE academic programs and student interests. Expanding employer participation in CBE internships through ongoing outreach and engagement activities. Representing CBE at employer, university, industry, and community events related to internships, workforce development, and professionalism. Facilitating experiential learning opportunities (e.g., case competitions, industry projects) with employers to advance relationship management. Monitoring workforce trends and employer needs to identify emerging opportunities for students. 25% - Support academic programs that utilize required internships. Collaborating with departmental and program leadership to align internship opportunities with program requirements, learning objectives, and workforce needs. Developing dedicated internship opportunities that sufficiently support program-specific requirements. Cultivating and maintaining employer partnerships that support required internship programs. Tracking and reporting on students in internships meeting programmatic needs. 15% - Conduct activities that connect students with internship opportunities. Coordinating with the CBE Undergraduate Career Services Manager and WWU Career Center members as appropriate to ensure students are professionally prepared for internship opportunities. Developing and disseminating internship communications, employer information, and related internship resources through various media channels. Contributing events to the CBE Career Calendar. Running internship information sessions, employer presentations, internship fairs, and similar student outreach activities. Facilitating connections between students and employers through recruiting, networking, internship promotion, and related activities. Serving as a resource to students, employers, and faculty regarding internship opportunities, expectations, and processes. 10% - Provide internship reporting, assessment, and strategic support to advance CBE internship goals. Meeting periodically with CBE leadership, faculty, departments, and university partners regarding internship and experiential learning initiatives. Tracking and reporting internship development activities, employer engagement, student participation, and related outcomes. Preparing reports that include analysis, highlights, and recommendations regarding internship participation, employer engagement, and overall program effectiveness. Providing and supporting necessary CBE reporting related to student success, workforce readiness, internship participation, accreditation, and strategic planning efforts. Required Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree in business, Human Resources, Communications, Public Administration, Economic Development, Higher Education, or a related field. Equivalent combinations of education and experience may be considered. Three years of professional experience in employer relations, recruiting, workforce development, internship programs, business development, career services, economic development, or a closely related field. Demonstrated ability to build and maintain productive relationships with employers, alumni, community partners, faculty, students, and other stakeholders. Demonstrated ability to develop, coordinate, and manage programs, projects, partnerships, or initiatives involving multiple constituents. Strong communication, presentation, and interpersonal skills. Strong organizational, project management, and time-management skills, including the ability to manage multiple priorities and deadlines. Knowledge of internship, experiential learning, workforce development, recruiting, or talent development practices relevant to undergraduate business students. Preferred Qualifications Master\u2019s degree in business administration, Human Resources, Organizational Leadership, Higher Education, Public Administration, or a related field. Five or more years of progressively responsible experience in employer relations, recruiting, workforce development, internship development, business development, career services, or a related field. Experience working with undergraduate students in a higher education setting. Experience developing partnerships with employers, alumni, professional associations, advisory boards, or community organizations. Experience supporting internship, experiential learning, workforce development, or work-based learning initiatives. Experience using relationship management, internship management, CRM, career services, or other stakeholder engagement technologies. Experience collecting, analyzing, and reporting data to assess program effectiveness and support continuous improvement. Knowledge of regional workforce needs, employer hiring trends, and experiential learning best practices. Understanding of emerging technologies, including artificial intelligence, and their impact on workforce development, recruiting, and internship opportunities. Special Requirements Must be willing to do some travel (for example, meeting employers in Bellingham/Seattle/Surrounding areas, taking students to meet employers, etc.) Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Hiring range is $33,846 - $39,923/year (@.6/10-months). Through longevity position tops out at $43,999/year. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Administrative Professional Position . Application Instructions Application materials should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Please submit a resume and cover letter that details how you meet the minimum and preferred requirements of the position, include 3 professional references (contacted only for applicants that make the finalist stage). Closing Date Notes Application review begins October 30, 2026; position is open until filled. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503237 Advertised: Oct 02 2026 08:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503238/undergraduate-career-services-manager-college-of-business-and-economics": {"title": "Undergraduate Career Services Manager, College of Business and Economics", "description": "Undergraduate Career Services Manager, College of Business and Economics Apply now Work type: Permanent Full Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Business and Economics Position Title Undergraduate Career Services Manager, College of Business and Economics About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of Business and Economics supports Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. All are encouraged to apply including women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. We are a student-centered community of scholars and professionals dedicated to providing students with the foundation necessary to have a lifelong positive impact on business and society.\u200b About the Position The primary purpose of this position is to provide professional career development services for students enrolled in undergraduate programs in the College of Business and Economics (CBE). Through student career advising and events, this position helps students explore career interests, build skills, and connect with post-graduate opportunities for employment and graduate school outcomes. Working collaboratively with students, faculty, alumni, employers, campus partners, and the business community, the position supports student success and career readiness in alignment with the CBE Strategic Plan and AACSB accreditation expectations. Timely career placement is essential to the mission of the college, and this position plays an important role in helping students achieve their professional goals and serve as change agents to society. This is an in-person, on-campus position located at Western Washington University in Bellingham, Washington. Position Duties and Responsibilities 35% - Design, coordinate, and deliver career-related programs, services, resources, and events that support the professional development of CBE undergraduate students. Planning and facilitating career-related programming and initiatives. Examples may include employer information sessions, employer site visits, networking events, speed networking events, etiquette seminars, interview preparation workshops, mentorship activities, and similar programs. Maintaining a CBE career activities calendar that is shared with students. Creating and disseminating career-related communications and social media content. Developing student resources, handouts, templates, and trainings for career exploration and job preparation. Designing pathways that help students learn about and utilize career technology resources and career management platforms. Actively engage in CBE career event promotions and career engagement and outcome tracking. Supporting undergraduate career mentorship initiatives of the Dean\u2019s Office. Aligning and supporting other CBE career activities as appropriate (e.g., Professional Career Readiness (PCR) Program, departmental initiatives, etc.). 35% - Provide individualized and group career advising that helps CBE students explore, prepare for, and pursue employment, internship, and graduate school opportunities. Providing scheduled and drop-in career advising services. This may include r\u00e9sum\u00e9 and cover letter preparation. Assisting students with career exploration. Preparing students for internship and job searches, graduate school planning, interviewing, networking, and other job skills. Helping students develop and implement personalized plans for successful post-graduation outcomes. Supporting relevant student opportunities for peer career advising, career ambassador initiatives, and other student career development efforts. Maintaining current knowledge of workforce trends, employer needs, career development best practices, and NACE career readiness competencies. 10% - Provide college and university partners with data and recommendations to advance CBE student career success and college career targets. Meeting periodically with CBE leadership, faculty, staff, student organizations, campus-wide Career Services, and other university partners on career-related initiatives. Tracking and reporting career outcomes quarterly that culminates in an annual report. The report should include analysis, highlights, and recommendations about improvements toward graduate school placements, student participation, employer engagement, and overall program effectiveness. Providing and supporting necessary CBE reporting related to student success, career readiness, and career outcomes for accreditation and strategic planning purposes. 20% - Develop and maintain partnerships with stakeholders that expand CBE student success opportunities for employment, internship, mentoring, networking, and experiential learning opportunities. Building and maintaining relationships with key WWU internal units and CBE external communities to engage employers, alumni, advisory board members, professional associations, study abroad programs, and community partners. Assisting where appropriate in the identification and the cultivation of internship, employment, mentoring, networking, and experiential learning opportunities aligned with CBE academic programs and student interests. Representing CBE Career Services at employer, university, and community events. Facilitating connections between students and employers through recruiting, networking, mentoring, and other career-related activities. Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree in Business, Student Affairs, Counseling, Human Resources, Communications, or a related field. Equivalent combinations of education and experience may be considered. Three years of professional experience in career services, recruiting, employer relations, student advising, workforce development, human resources, student affairs, or a closely related field. Demonstrated ability to advise, coach, mentor, or support individuals in achieving educational, professional, or career-related goals. Demonstrated ability to build relationships and communicate effectively with students, employers, alumni, faculty, staff, and community partners. Strong organizational, project management, and time-management skills, including the ability to manage multiple priorities and deadlines. Knowledge of business career pathways and employment opportunities relevant to undergraduate business students. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in Business Administration, Student Affairs, Counseling, Human Resources, Organizational Leadership, or a related field. Five or more years of progressively responsible experience in career services, recruiting, employer relations, student advising, workforce development, or a related field. Experience working with undergraduate students in a higher education setting. Experience developing and facilitating programs, workshops, events, mentoring programs, or employer engagement initiatives. Experience building partnerships with employers, alumni, professional associations, or community organizations. Experience using career management systems, mentoring platforms, CRM systems, student success technologies, or similar technology resources. Experience collecting, analyzing, and reporting data to assess program effectiveness and support continuous improvement. Familiarity with NACE Career Readiness Competencies, career development best practices, and current employment trends. [naceweb.org], [sunypoly.edu] Understanding of emerging technologies, including artificial intelligence, and their impact on career development, recruiting, and workforce readiness. Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Hiring range is $67,692 - $77,846/year depending on qualifications and experience. Through longevity position tops out at $87,999. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Administrative Professional Position . Application Instructions Application materials should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Applicants must submit a r\u00e9sum\u00e9 and cover letter that clearly address how they meet the minimum and preferred qualifications for the position. Application materials should also include contact information for three professional references. References will be contacted only for finalists. Closing Date Notes Application review begins October 30, 2026; position is open until filled. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503238 Advertised: Oct 02 2026 08:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503202/grant-and-contract-coordinator": {"title": "Grant and Contract Coordinator", "description": "Grant and Contract Coordinator Apply now Work type: Project Full Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: Academic Affairs Position Title Grant and Contract Coordinator About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The Office of Research and Sponsored Programs supports Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. For more information about our department please visit our website: Research and Sponsored Programs Office | Office of the Vice Provost for Research | Western Washington University About the Position At Western Washington University, research administration professionals help faculty and staff turn ideas into funded projects that expand knowledge, engage students, and serve our communities. This position offers the opportunity to contribute directly to WWU\u2019s growing research enterprise while working in a collaborative environment that values integrity, clarity, continuous learning, and responsive service. We are looking for a detail-oriented, service-minded professional who can balance compliance, customer service, and problem-solving in a complex university environment. The strongest candidates will bring excellent communication skills, sound judgment, comfort with financial analysis and monitoring, and the ability to explain policies and sponsor requirements clearly to diverse audiences. Key Responsibilities Pre-Award Administration : Advise faculty on funding opportunities and sponsor requirements; interpret RFPs/FOAs; develop and review proposal budgets, justifications, and cost-share commitments; coordinate proposal components, including subrecipient documentation; manage internal routing and approvals; ensure submission compliance and timeliness; support faculty in developing competitive proposals and identifying funding sources; and develop and support workshops on grant development. Post-Award Administration : Coordinate award setup, including account creation, budgets, and project initiation; monitor expenditures for allowability, allocability, and compliance with sponsor requirements; perform account reconciliation, financial analysis, and budget projections; advise PIs on financial status and compliance requirements; process and support post-award actions such as rebudgeting, no-cost extensions, and PI changes; monitor cost share, effort reporting, and subrecipient compliance; coordinate with finance, payroll, and other administrative units; and ensure timely financial reporting and award closeout. Contract and Subaward Administration : Assist with review, negotiation, and execution of subawards and contracts; support data use agreements and sponsored consulting agreements; ensure agreements align with sponsor requirements and university policies; and coordinate with central offices and external partners on agreement execution. Advising, Compliance, and Collaboration : Serve as a primary contact for faculty and staff engaged in biomedical research; stay up to date on federal funder policies, procedures, and notices; interpret and apply federal regulations, including Uniform Guidance / 2 CFR 200; provide training and guidance on research administration processes and funder compliance requirements; and collaborate across departments to resolve complex issues. This is a project position, ending no later than September 15, 2028 per university guidelines. Anticipated continued funding through 2031 is based on grant award documents. Required Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree or equivalent combination of education and experience. 2\u20133+ years of experience in grants management, research administration, or financial administration. Knowledge of federal, state, and sponsor regulations (e.g., 2 CFR 200 Uniform Guidance). Strong analytical, organizational, and problem-solving skills. Ability to interpret and apply complex procedures, policies and regulations Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Strong customer service orientation and collaborative approach. Ability to manage multiple priorities with attention to detail and deadlines. Proficiency with Microsoft Office and ability to learn enterprise systems. Preferred Qualifications 3+ years of pre- and post-award research administration experience. Experience in a higher education or research-intensive environment. Certified Research Administrator (CRA) or similar credential. Experience with grants management systems (e.g., Banner, Streamlyne, Workday, or similar). Experience with contract and subaward administration. Demonstrated experience in process improvement or data analysis Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary $4,772 - $6,415/month depending on qualifications and experience Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Classified Positions Bargaining Unit This position is part of bargaining unit PT and is covered by the collective bargaining agreement with Public School Employees of WWU. Application Instructions Application materials should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Closing Date Notes Application review begins October 28, 2026; position is open until filled. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503202 Advertised: Sep 30 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503259/associate-vp-for-information-technology-services-chief-information-officer": {"title": "Associate VP for Information Technology Services & Chief Information Officer", "description": "Associate VP for Information Technology Services & Chief Information Officer Apply now Work type: Permanent Full Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: Business and Financial Affairs Position Title Associate VP for Information Technology Services & Chief Information Officer About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The division of Business and Financial Affairs supports Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. All are encouraged to apply, including women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. For more information about Information Technology Services, visit https://its.wwu.edu and the Division of Business and Financial Affairs at: https://bfa.wwu.edu/ . About the Position The Associate Vice President for Information Technology Services/Chief Information Officer provides strategic leadership for all aspects of the university\u2019s information technology, including infrastructure, software, and user services. The AVP ITS/CIO provides strategic planning for technology at Western, provides leadership, and fosters teamwork across all Information Technology Services (ITS) units, which comprise more than 100 employees. The AVP ITS/CIO supervises Academic Technology and User Services, Enterprise Application Services, Enterprise Infrastructure Services, and the Information Security Office. The AVP ITS/CIO must manage, inspire, and lead a diverse team of dedicated professionals and support staff. They foster and sustain a workplace that promotes and values accessibility, equity, inclusion, and diversity. Understanding key performance metrics and implementing effective strategies to improve service and project delivery are essential. Clear, persuasive, and proactive communication is critical to achieving ITS's strategic goals. The ideal candidate is a visionary leader with extensive experience in information technology and a passion for driving innovation to advance student success. Opportunities and Expectations for Leadership The AVP ITS/CIO will join Western Washington University at an exciting time. Western launched a guaranteed admissions program in 2026 and expanded it to include all Washington residents for 2027 offering a direct path to a highly rated public university in the Pacific Northwest. We will be launching a major campaign in the coming year with a focus on scholarships and support for a new College of Health Sciences. We are also moving to a new Enterprise Resource Planning tool with implementation beginning in summer of 2027. Recognizing the challenges facing our society, the university has developed an ambitious strategic plan to advance inclusive student success, increase impact in Washington State and beyond, and enhance academic excellence. To that end, the successful candidate will bring innovative solutions to the following areas of opportunity. Drive a vision of integrated service delivery. This is a unique opportunity to foster a cohesive unit that provides integrated services to the university. This will require the ability to engage various employee groups and enhance a culture of mutual respect and trust, continuous improvement, transitional change, and operational excellence. A demonstrated track record of integrity and success leading organizations through transformative improvement, including the ability to originate change, deliver results, and foster a continuous improvement environment, is key. The successful candidate will ensure the unit's services are supported by the most appropriate systems, processes, and tools. The AVP ITS/CIO must have deep familiarity with AI trends, IT governance, cloud computing, and enterprise infrastructure. Demonstrated knowledge of cybersecurity risk management, data protection, and regulatory compliance methods, practices, and procedures, along with experience developing and monitoring a large IT budget with a focus on optimizing operational costs, is also essential. Foster a culture of collaboration. The AVP ITS/CIO must have strong interpersonal skills, the ability to influence at all levels within the university and local community, and the capacity to function as an effective change agent. The successful candidate will model a collaborative way of working, both within the unit and with university stakeholders. This means that the individual must be very effective at cultivating and establishing trust and credibility with others. The successful candidate will model and promote a culture where accessibility, diversity, inclusion, and equity are highly valued and integrated in information technology operations. Lead efforts to advance a Culture of Care and Open Accountability, which are fundamental principles integral to the success of Western Washington University. The division conducted a culture and climate assessment in 2022, which led to a report outlining the steps needed to develop and maintain a Culture of Care and Open Accountability . The successful candidate will be expected to continue this work throughout the IT unit. They should be able to demonstrate a record of fostering an equitable workplace that includes internal and external stakeholders, with advocacy on behalf of all constituents, including individuals from different cultural backgrounds and social identities. Demonstrate a strong commitment to innovation and best practices. The AVP ITS/CIO will be a results-driven leader who can assess current systems, resources, practices, and processes to develop a vision for a modern and forward-looking technology infrastructure. The successful candidate will foster an environment of strategic thinking and apply data-based decision-making. They will need to establish metrics to generate baselines and implement tools to evaluate results and assess impact. They will need to build and measure key performance indicators and share outcomes with staff to identify areas for improvement. Western is implementing a new ERP, Workday, over the next three years. Implementation for Finance, Payroll, and Human Resources will begin in July 2027, with go-live in summer 2028. Implementation for the Student platform will begin in Fall 2028, with go-live in Fall 2029. The successful candidate will be pivotal in driving Western's operational excellence strategy through the ERP implementation. The AVP ITS/CIO will lead digital transformation and provide recommendations on using AI in operational software that aligns with Western\u2019s values. Required Qualifications Minimum of 10 years of progressively responsible management and leadership experience in information technology and information security. An earned bachelor\u2019s degree from an accredited institution, or international equivalent. Experience in preparing and maintaining complex budgets. An understanding of the values and principles that guide public higher education. Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, including demonstrated experience. Ability to communicate effectively with both technical and non-technical audiences, with a strong customer service focus. Successful experience supervising technology personnel, including leading, managing, coaching, and developing those individuals utilizing methodologies for continuous improvement. Demonstrated leadership skills in collaborative decision-making and building constructive professional relationships across organizational units. Demonstrated experience in strategic planning and execution of information technology solutions within a complex environment. Demonstrates a commitment to and experience in championing diversity, equity, and inclusion by fostering a collaborative environment where every voice is heard and valued. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree from an accredited institution or international equivalent, in a discipline relevant to this position. Experience as a senior/executive leader in a complex information technology organization. Significant experience and skill in negotiating with vendors, contractors, and others. Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. This position requires a fingerprint background check. Salary Hiring range is $205,363 - $236,168/year depending on qualifications and experience. Through longevity position tops out at $266,972/year. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Administrative Professional Position . Application Instructions Application materials should include: Letter of interest that addresses how the candidate\u2019s experiences match the position requirements. Resume or curriculum vitae. Five professional references (references will not be contacted without prior permission from the applicant). Western Washington University is being assisted with this search by Another Source. To apply, please visit: https://careers-anothersource.icims.com/jobs/102572/associate-vice-president-for-information-technology-services-and-chief-information-officer---western-washington-university/job Nominators and prospective candidates may also arrange a confidential conversation about this opportunity with Jessica Kingery, Senior Talent Strategist, jessicak@anothersource.com Closing Date Notes Position closes 10/25/2026. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503259 Advertised: Sep 30 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Oct 25 2026 23:55 Pacific Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503222/decision-sciences-nontenure-track-faculty-vacancy-pool": {"title": "Decision Sciences - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool", "description": "Decision Sciences - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool Apply now Work type: NTT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Business and Economics Position Title Decision Sciences - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of Business and Economics and the Decision Sciences Department support Western's mission, which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. The mission of the Department of Decision Sciences is to advance and disseminate knowledge in management information systems, manufacturing and supply chain management, and business analytics. We provide high quality educational programs, publish theoretical and applied research, and serve University, professional, and community organizations. About the Position The Decision Sciences Department invites applications for a vacancy pool from which candidates will be selected to fill full and part-time, non-tenure track positions. Positions may become available at any time and are typically filled as they become available. Position Responsibilities: The department, college, and University value teaching, and expect Instructors to be excellent teachers; to be current in their discipline; to engage students actively in learning through discussion, writing, and analysis; and to set high standards regarding course content. Instructors are expected to provide students with feedback on their coursework and examinations and to be available to students through regularly scheduled office hours in keeping with department policy. Instructors must have their students evaluate their course(s) using the standard University evaluation forms, or others approved by the provost and must make those evaluations available to the department chairs and faculty for annual review. Required Qualifications MBA or master's degree in appropriate field Evidence of effective teaching skills and performance Academically or professionally qualified in accordance with college and AACSB standards. AACSB qualification status is based on the applicant\u2019s record with respect to factors including academic preparation, scholarly publication record, professional work experience, and ongoing engagement with academic and professional activities. Ability to work effectively with diverse students, faculty, and staff and to teach an increasingly diverse student population that includes students traditionally underrepresented in business fields. Preferred Qualifications PhD in appropriate field Evidence of teaching experience and effective teaching performance at the undergraduate level Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Commensurate with experience and qualifications Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions A cover letter and curriculum vitae are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and each of the required and preferred qualifications, including your ability to work effectively with diverse students, faculty, and staff and to teach an increasingly diverse student population that includes students traditionally underrepresented in business fields. Additional material required as outlined below. Required Supplemental Materials As part of the application, you will be asked to provide the names and contact information of three references. If reference letters are needed, the PageUp online application system will contact your three references to request upload of reference letters. Additional Information This vacancy pool is refreshed annually; please see applicable close date information below. Applicants who continue to remain interested in being considered for on-going opportunities should re-submit updated materials to the new pool, once posted. Applicant materials are reviewed on as-needed basis. You will only be contacted if an opportunity is available. Closing Date Notes Applications are accepted continuously for potential NTT faculty openings; please apply at your earliest convenience. Vacancy pool closes no later than September 30, 2027 at 11:55 pm. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503222 Advertised: Sep 25 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Sep 30 2027 23:55 Pacific Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503230/director-child-development-center": {"title": "Director Child Development Center", "description": "Director Child Development Center Apply now Work type: Permanent Full Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: Business and Financial Affairs Position Title Director Child Development Center About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The Child Development Center (CDC) supports Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. The CDC provides high-quality early care and education for children ages 12 months to 5 years. We participate in Washington State\u2019s Early Achievers Quality Rating and Improvement Program and are committed to supporting each child\u2019s social-emotional, physical, and cognitive development in a safe and nurturing environment. Our mission is to partner with children, families, and students in promoting the holistic development of every child within the CDC community. About the Position The CDC Director provides strategic and collaborative leadership to advance the CDC in support of the mission of Western Washington University, the division of Business and Financial Affairs (BFA), and the unit of Risk, Ethics, Safety, Resilience, and Enterprise Operations (RESR-EO). The Director oversees a high-quality early childhood education program serving WWU students, faculty, staff, and a limited number of alumni and community families. The Director is responsible for all aspects of CDC operations, including personnel and budget management, enrollment, strategic planning, regulatory compliance, staff development, and program assessment. The Director leads a team of early childhood educators and collaborates with university departments, campus partners, families, and external agencies to support program excellence and continuous improvement. Committed to providing developmentally appropriate early learning experiences, the CDC also serves as a valuable teaching and learning partner through its collaboration with the Woodring College of Education, supporting practicum, internship, employment, and research opportunities for future educators. The Director advances program sustainability through strategic growth, partnerships, and other funding opportunities. Reporting to the AVP for RESR-EO, the Director ensures compliance with Washington State licensing and quality standards, including participation in the Early Achievers program. The CDC currently serves 87 children ages 1\u20135 across six classrooms, and the Director provides leadership to sustain and strengthen the program's long-term success. Position Duties and Responsibilities Leadership, Personnel Management & Organizational Culture (25%) Provides leadership for all personnel and organizational development activities to ensure a positive, inclusive, welcoming, and family-centered environment. Recruit, hire, onboard, supervise, mentor, evaluate, and retain a diverse and inclusive workforce, including classified staff, substitutes, volunteers, interns, and practicum students. Collaborate with and provide oversight to the Operations Manager regarding the recruitment, onboarding, supervision, and development of student employees. Foster a collaborative and supportive workplace culture that promotes employee engagement, effective communication, shared leadership, and organizational effectiveness. Manage personnel matters in accordance with university policies, collective bargaining agreements, and applicable regulations, including conducting annual performance evaluations, supporting employee development through coaching and mentoring, and implementing performance improvement strategies as needed. Ensure staff maintain required certifications, professional development hours, and licensing qualifications, while promoting continuous learning and growth in early childhood education, leadership, equity, inclusion, and best practices. Program Administration, Licensing, Compliance & Risk Management (20%) Provides oversight of operational systems, regulatory compliance, risk management, and continuous quality improvement to ensure effective and compliant program administration. Ensure compliance with all applicable federal, state, university, and licensing requirements governing childcare operations, including maintaining program licensing through the Washington State Department of Children, Youth, and Families (DCYF), participation in Washington's Quality Rating and Improvement System (QRIS) through Early Achievers, and serving as the responsible principal for the USDA Child and Adult Care Food Program (CACFP). Develop, implement, and evaluate policies, procedures, systems, and operational practices that support program quality, accountability, equitable access, and continuous improvement. Ensure the confidentiality, security, and appropriate management of sensitive information, records, and program documentation in accordance with applicable laws and university policies. Lead emergency preparedness, health and safety, and risk management efforts, collaborating with university partners including Risk Management, Environmental Health & Safety, Human Resources, and other campus offices to promote compliance and operational excellence. Fiscal Management & Business Operations (15%) As financial manager, maintain a current understanding of the financial situation of assigned funds and adhere to the financial manager responsibilities as defined in university policy POL-U5320.03. Ensure that funds are spent, obligated, and/or committed in accordance with all university policies and procedures, standards, state and federal rules, and applicable grant and contract language, and within available budget. This may include developing spending plans and forecasts for managed funds, oversight of individuals with delegated spending authority, and additional training on financial principles. Oversee individuals who have been delegated as a Budget Authority to ensure that these individuals understand the policies and procedures and that their expenditures are within budget and compliant with university standards. Develop and oversee annual operating budgets, long-range financial plans, and fiscal strategies in collaboration with the Operations Manager and Western Washington University's Business & Financial Affairs (BFA) unit. Monitor revenues, expenditures, enrollment trends, and program performance to support informed decision-making and sustainable operations. Oversee the financial management, including multiple funding sources such as tuition revenue, grants, donor funds, subsidies, and university resources. Provide oversight and direction to the Operations Manager regarding billing, collections, contracts, purchasing, payroll coordination, and financial reporting processes. Analyze financial data, assess fiscal risks and opportunities, and ensure compliance with university fiscal policies, state regulations, and applicable funding requirements. Implement corrective actions and operational adjustments as needed to maintain fiscal health. Support long-range planning and university initiatives by evaluating the financial feasibility of future program enhancements, service expansions, facility improvements, and other strategic opportunities. Educational Leadership, Program Quality & Assessment (15%) Provides leadership to ensure high-quality early learning experiences through curriculum development, instructional support, continuous assessment, and evidence-based practices. Support teachers in developing and implementing developmentally appropriate, inclusive, and research-based curriculum that reflects current best practices and maintains excellence in inquiry-based learning. Ensure staff have access to the resources, assessment tools, and professional support needed to deliver high-quality educational experiences. Establish and oversee systems for assessing program quality, child outcomes, and organizational effectiveness to support continuous improvement and informed decision-making. Foster inclusive learning environments that support the diverse needs of children and families while promoting evidence-based teaching practices and ongoing professional growth. Collaborate with university faculty and academic programs to strengthen the CDC's role as a model early learning environment, integrating emerging research and best practices into curriculum, instruction, and program development. Enrollment, Family Engagement & Communications (15%) Provides leadership for enrollment management, family engagement, and communications to support equitable access, transparency, and strong partnerships with families and the university community. Provide strategic oversight to the Operations Manager in monitoring enrollment, waitlists, retention, and access to services, while ensuring enrollment priorities among students, faculty, staff, and community families align with the CDC's mission, university priorities, and applicable laws. Ensure timely, accurate, and accessible communication with families and stakeholders regarding program operations, enrollment, policies, and services. Oversee public-facing communications, including website content, publications, tours, presentations, and other informational materials that promote awareness of the CDC and its programs. Foster meaningful partnerships with families by developing systems and practices that encourage engagement, collaboration, and open communication in support of children's learning and well-being. University & Community Partnerships, Strategic Planning & Advocacy (10%) Provides leadership for strategic planning, partnership development, and community engagement to advance the mission of the Child Development Center, Western Washington University, and the unit of Risk, Ethics, Safety, Resilience, and Enterprise Operations (RESR-EO). Lead strategic planning initiatives by assessing current and emerging childcare needs, conducting program evaluations and stakeholder engagement, and identifying opportunities to strengthen services and support the evolving needs of the university community. Build and sustain collaborative partnerships with university departments, academic programs, community organizations, public agencies, and professional associations. Collaborate with partners such as the Woodring College of Education, Whatcom Early Learning Alliance, Bellingham Public Schools, Whatcom Community College, the Washington Coalition for Campus Children's Centers, and other organizations that support early childhood education, student learning, and family engagement. Represent the Child Development Center on university committees, community partnerships, and professional organizations, ensuring the CDC's priorities align with the strategic goals of Western Washington University and the RESR-EO/Business & Financial Affairs unit. Promote awareness of childcare resources and advocate for high-quality early childhood education and family support services. Foster opportunities for student learning, research, internships, practicum placements, and community engagement while maintaining collaborative relationships that support continuous program quality and participation in state quality initiatives, including Early Achievers. Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree in Early Childhood Education, Child Development, Early Education Leadership, or related field. Progressive leadership experience in an early childhood education, childcare, educational, or human services setting, including experience supervising, mentoring, and developing staff. Demonstrated experience with child development, early childhood education best practices, developmentally appropriate practice, and inclusive learning environments for children aged 1-5 yrs old. Demonstrated ability to build collaborative relationships with families, staff, university partners, and community stakeholders. Demonstrated commitment to fostering equity, inclusion, accessibility, and belonging through leadership and organizational practices. Demonstrated strong communication, organizational, problem-solving, and technology skills, including proficiency with standard business and productivity software. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in Early Childhood Education, Child Development, Early Education Leadership, or related field. Experience directing or managing a licensed childcare center, preferably in a higher education or university-affiliated setting. Experience with Washington State childcare licensing regulations, DCYF requirements, and quality improvement systems, including Early Achievers. Experience with budget development, financial planning, resource management, and overseeing multiple funding sources, including grants, contracts, donor funds, tuition revenue, or public subsidies. Experience with strategic planning, organizational change, continuous quality improvement, and program assessment. Experience collaborating with colleges or universities to support practicum students, interns, research, or educator preparation programs. Knowledge of the unique needs of student parents, university communities, and family-centered early learning programs. Special Requirements Ability to obtain and maintain an active account in the DCYF MERIT Workforce Registry and complete all required state preservice and ongoing training requirements. Successful completion of a DCYF background check prior to employment. Ability to obtain and maintain all required health and safety certifications and clearances upon hire, including Pediatric CPR/First Aid, Bloodborne Pathogens training, and TB testing, in accordance with DCYF licensing requirements. Ability to complete and maintain all required DCYF trainings, including Child Care Basics and a minimum of 10 hours of leadership-focused in-service training every 36 months. Ability to meet and maintain all Washington Administrative Code (WAC) and DCYF licensing requirements applicable to Child Care Center Directors throughout employment. Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Hiring range is $90,714 - $104,323/year depending on qualifications and experience. Through longevity position tops out at $117,928/year. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Administrative Professional Position . Application Instructions A cover letter and resume are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Please include the names and contact information of three professional references. References will only be contacted if necessary, with notice provided beforehand. Closing Date Notes Application review begins October 26, 2026; position is open until filled. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503230 Advertised: Sep 24 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503099/mba-career-services-manager": {"title": "MBA Career Services Manager", "description": "MBA Career Services Manager Apply now Work type: Permanent Part Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Business and Economics Position Title MBA Career Services Manager About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of Business Graduate Programs supports Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. The College of Business and Economics at WWU is a student-centered community of scholars and professionals dedicated to providing students with the foundation necessary to have a lifelong positive impact on business and society. About the Position The primary purpose of this position is to provide career development opportunities for students enrolled in Graduate Programs in the College of Business and Economics (CBE), specifically those enrolled in the Masters of Business Administration (MBA) program and to a lesser degree Masters of Professional Accounting (MPACC) program. By designing career related programming, offering one-on-one counseling and advising services, and hosting events for students, the role supports graduate students in fulfilling career related requirements of their program, and meeting their individual employment goals as they near graduation. When not working with students directly, the person in this position will work closely with relevant employers in the Northwest region to foster employment and internship opportunities for students, as well as engage regularly with the MBA Operations team, the campus-wide Career Services office, and their CBE Career Services colleagues. Career placement at graduation is essential to the mission of the programs, and the career services office provides an integral support role in helping our students reach their goals, and ultimately has a great deal of influence on their overall satisfaction when they graduate. This is a permanent, part-time (.5FTE) position. Position Duties and Responsibilities 35% - Designing, directing, and delivering programs, services, and events specific to graduate student career services in CBE. Planning special events for students and employers related to MBA career services. Determine, assign, and review work of Graduate Assistant working on behalf of MBA Program Career Services. Maintain documentation regarding student placements and report data to MBA Operations team and campus-wide Career Services office. Manage MBA Mentorship Program which helps to connect current students with MBA alumni, relevant faculty, and other targeted working professionals in the student's area of interest. 35% - Tailored career and internship planning with graduate students in CBE. Hosting group and one-on-one career counseling sessions with current students to help them plan strategic internship choices, and act as point person for all internship related requirements and paperwork (proper submittal of online proposal, employer evaluations, and final paper). Help students develop a plan for successful job placement upon graduation from the MBA Program. Role also includes developing and fostering internship pipelines that mesh with the MBA student schedules, which involves regular connections with hiring stakeholders (HR professionals, alumni, Foundation, etc.). 10% - Integrated work with the MBA Operations team, campus-wide Career Services Offices and CBE Career Support Team. Attend regularly scheduled MBA Operations team meetings, quarterly MBA Advisory Board Meetings, and report monthly to Faculty Curriculum Committee on matters related to MBA Career Services. Attend regularly scheduled meetings with campus and college-wide Career Services to help maintain connection to campus-wide resources and events. 10% - Meet with and foster relationships with external stakeholders. Building and maintaining relationships with MBA alumni, faculty members, advisory board members, University partners (Outreach and Continuing Education, departments offering internships, etc.), regional employers, outside partners (SHRM for example), and other local organizations for the benefit of our current students and their career aspirations. Hosting or co-hosting events as the career services representative. 10% - Additional duties/responsibilities Lead Advisor for MBA Association (MBA student club); Help reviewing MBA applicants as needed (specifically their work experience and work prospects); Assisting MPACC students in fall quarter under guidance from the Accounting Department; Leading Career Development subcommittee for the MBA Program Advisory Board (1-2 times per year); engagement in all MBA orientation sessions when new cohorts are launched; Support for MBA Lunch and Learn and Speaker Series Required Qualifications Minimum of five years of professional experience in Career Services, Human Resources, Recruiting, Student Advising or other relevant field Bachelor\u2019s degree (additional work experience beyond the minimum 5 years may substitute for the Bachelor\u2019s degree requirement) Strong advising and coaching skills Ability to conduct presentations - both internally and externally and using relevant technology Ability to professionally represent WWU to external and internal stakeholders Excellent organization and time-management skills Technological proficiency online and with common software (such as Zoom, MS Teams, Powerpoint, Excel or other data visualization tools) Understanding of AI and how it impacts students in their job search Ability to work with and advise students that have varying levels of professional work experience Preferred Qualifications MBA or other Masters degree Prior experience in advising/coaching professionals or Masters level students Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Hiring range is $33,846 - $38,923 (@.50) depending on qualifications and experience. Through longevity position tops out at $43,999/year. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Administrative Professional Position . Application Instructions A cover letter and resume are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Please include the names and contact information of three professional references. References will only be contacted if necessary, with notice provided beforehand. Closing Date Notes Application review begins October 19, 2026;position is open until filled. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503099 Advertised: Sep 17 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503224/associate-director-of-financial-aid-systems-and-operations": {"title": "Associate Director of Financial Aid Systems and Operations", "description": "Associate Director of Financial Aid Systems and Operations Apply now Work type: Permanent Full Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: Academic Affairs Position Title Associate Director of Financial Aid Systems and Operations About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The Financial Aid Department supports Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. About the Position The Associate Director of Financial Aid Systems and Operations has high level leadership responsibility for Western Washington University, directing the Systems and Operations enterprises for Financial Aid. The Associate Director reports to the Executive Director of Financial Aid (ED-Fin Aid), providing strategic administration of student financial aid programs through the effective implementation, configuration, and management of financial aid software for the Financial Aid department and by oversight of application processing flow, systems documentation, and staff training. The Associate Director supervises the Assistant Director for Financial Aid Operations, one IT Business Analyst, and one Senior Financial Aid Counselor, and indirectly supervises three classified program coordinators and one program assistant who serve within the Financial Aid Operations Center. Additionally, the Associate Director coordinates with Enterprise and Application Services. The Associate Director completes reports and surveys with significant compliance implications for the institution. This leader oversees administration of a number of financial aid programs, has significant responsibility for managing disbursement of millions of dollars of federal and state aid for the institution, and serves as a member of the Financial Aid Leadership Team. Duties / Responsibilities: Systems Team Leadership and Responsibility The position ensures the compliant, efficient processing of federal, state, and institutional aid and manages the department's technology strategy and systems integration. Key platforms include Ellucian Banner, FA Data Warehouse, OnBase, NextGen, and U.S. Department of Education software. Maintains an up-to-date understanding of the functions that financial aid software can provide through training and self-initiated research. Coordinates Ellucian Banner financial aid upgrade activities with Enterprise Application Services, Financial Aid staff members and others. Performs extensive yearly financial aid systems configuration functions and process flow evaluation and implementation. Writes detailed programming specifications for required financial aid forms and reports not provided by the Ellucian Banner or OnBase imaging systems. Serves as a liaison with Enterprise Application Services to test and implement desired programming. Determines whether the financial aid software systems meet federal and state aid regulations, with the assistance of relevant staff. Informs the Executive Director of major inconsistencies between regulatory requirements and what the systems provide. Recommends proposals for systems resolution to the ED-Fin Aid with rationale (pros/cons) for such recommendations. Serves as primary representative/liaison for the Financial Aid office on institution-wide and state-wide technology initiatives. Prepares and conducts systems training sessions for departmental staff. Coordinates timely documentation of policies and procedures related to using the financial aid software system, largely by departmental staff. Participates on relevant interdepartmental committees that address technical and data integrity issues affecting administrative and academic departments at WWU and provides input for scheduling system down time and purchase of relevant software. Writes database queries using several report writing tools to retrieve data from the financial aid system, as well as from other modules within the Western Student System, to support awarding of aid, problem solving, and statistical reporting. Oversees other statistical reporting such as: Common Data Set, national college surveys, Washington Student Achievement Council requests, and requests from constituents within the WWU community. Works with the Financial Aid staff to identify and resolve system and process flow problems. Responsible for security administration for Financial Aid, including maintenance of access and security levels for all Ellucian Banner financial aid forms, OnBase financial aid access, the Washington Student Achievement Council secure website and portal and Federal Title IV web access (COD, CPS and NSLDS). Leads the Operations enterprise for Financial Aid. The Operations Team conducts the processing of 19,000+ active student financial aid applications, including student FAFSA retrieval, loading data to the Ellucian Banner financial aid system, grouping and batch processing, intake and upload of documentation to the electronic student files in the OnBase imaging system, student notifications and systems tracking of student documentation requirements, batch packaging of financial aid awards, cancellation of funding, transmittal and receipt of Federal Direct Loan, Pell Grant and Teach Grant records, processing PLUS Loan application matches and revisions, monitoring intersession activities and providing Satisfactory Academic Progress monitoring support. Supervision and Personnel Management Leads the Systems and Operations Center, which includes the Assistant Director for Financial Aid Operations, one Senior Financial Aid Counselor, and one IT Business Analyst, to provide a team approach to systems cross training, process flow analysis, and systems documentation. Directly Supervises one IT Business Analyst, who is responsible for serving as systems backup in the absence of the Associate Director, oversees financial aid data transmissions between Financial Aid and the Department of Education servicer, Argos report writing, and data security for the Financial Aid Department. Directly supervises the Assistant Director for Financial Aid Operations. Indirectly maintains oversight of the Operations Team, which includes three Program Coordinators, one Program Assistant and several student employees. The Assistant Director for Financial Aid Operations is a key member of the Systems Team and a leader in the Financial Aid Department, responsible for application process flow and systems configuration. Directly supervises one Senior Financial Aid Counselor position. This position's responsibilities include the regulatory oversight and reconciliation for the $17 million Federal Pell Grant program, $6 million private alternative loan program, $100,000 Alaska Loan program and time-sensitive financial aid withdrawal calculations and processing for federal, state, and institutional aid programs. In collaboration of Student and Faculty Systems in Enterprise Application Systems, helps coordinate the work of one IT Business Analyst. Assists with hiring authority for Systems and Operations Center and has significant responsibility in advising the Executive Director for Financial Aid as to the hiring, firing, advancement, promotion, or any other change of status of other Financial Aid employees. Reporting and Budget Responsibility Responsible for meeting high-stakes, high-risk reporting requirements on behalf of Western Washington University, defending and justifying our continued use of millions of dollars of federal and state aid. Oversees the production and filing of the annual State Unit Record report, which summarizes all aid received by Western Washington University students who received any funding during the year and the Federal IPEDS report. Oversees the accurate and timely compilation and filing of the FISAP, as well as quarterly and annual reports for funds including the Washington College Grant/College Bound Scholarship/Passport to College/Washington State Bridge Grant. These reports serve as requests for supplemental funding and substantiate disbursements to eligible students. Creates, runs, resolves and/or oversees discrepancy reports that ensure accurate delivery of aid, for example: Class level vs. Loan limits, Repeat Coursework Report, Late Starting Classes Report, Offered not Accepted Aid by Fund, and Accepted not Paid Aid by Fund. Oversees fund re-awarding projects and fund switching for reconciliation. Completes surveys including but not limited to IPEDS, the Common Data Set, U.S. News and World Report, Peterson's College Board-Big Future College, and Princeton Review. Serves as a member of the departmental Leadership Team, which plans and organizes department-wide work to ensure the accurate and timely delivery of financial aid to Western students. Collaborates with the Counseling Team and provides input for changes to review processes. Responsible for non-Title IV Federal emergency funding policy analysis, awarding, communications, and reporting. Maintains an up-to-date knowledge of awarding criteria associated with non-Title IV federal emergency funding and identifies students who are eligible for such funding via collaboration and systems retrievals among multiple areas, including but not limited to Financial Aid, the Registrar's Office, and Student Business Office. Provides clear communications to students that outline eligibility requirements and notifies students of their awards. For 2021-22, non-Title IV Federal emergency funding exceeded $16.1 million to an estimated 9,000 students and former students, from a population of approximately 26,000 eligible students and former students. Responsible for policy analysis, awarding and communications associated with administration of Washington State Bridge Grants, disbursed to approximately 2000 low-income students annually totaling an estimated $900,000. Budgetary responsibility for Aid Funds: Federal Pell Grant approximately $17 million to over 3,000 students annually; Federal Direct Loan Programs approximately $40 million to over 4,500 students annually; Washington State Grant/Scholarship Programs approximately $23 million to over 4,000 students annually. Miscellaneous Serves as a representative on inter-office committees and meetings not listed above, attends office and division meetings, monitors list-serve postings including but not limited to BFINAID, BPOST, BGENERAL, Ellucian E-Communities IFAP, and NASFAA, reads regulatory postings, attends trainings, conferences, and webinars, and completes other special projects as assigned by the Executive Director and/or ESS leadership. Performs other duties as assigned. Required Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s Degree Minimum 1 year of experience with support and maintenance of any Student Information System (SIS) Financial Aid Module Demonstrated experience working within Student Affairs/Services at a college or university Experience working in a college or university Financial Aid office Demonstrated experience learning and applying new technology Proven ability to maintain confidentiality and familiarity with the privacy and protection of sensitive data Demonstrated relational database experience, writing data queries and reports using query tools such as MS Access and SQL Developer Minimum 1 year supervisory or lead experience, with demonstrated ability to effectively plan, organize, prioritize, and delegate tasks to meet deadlines Demonstrated ability to accurately interpret and apply regulatory guidance to systems design Demonstrated commitment to support a workplace culture in which diversity and differing opinions are honored, respected, and celebrated Excellent communication, customer service and interpersonal skills Preferred Qualifications Master\u2019s Degree or 5+ years of direct, mission-critical software oversight within student affairs/services office(s) Proficiency with Ellucian\u2019s Banner Financial Aid module Working knowledge of OnBase or other imaging software Proficiency with Evisions Argos Report Writer or other report writers Project management experience Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Hiring range is $105,013 - $120,766/year depending on qualifications and experience. Through longevity position tops out at $136,517/year. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Administrative Professional Position . Application Instructions A cover letter and resume are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Please include the names and contact information of three professional references. References will only be contacted for finalists. Closing Date Notes Application review begins October 19, 2026; position is open until filled Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503224 Advertised: Sep 17 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/502905/musical-accompanist-modern-dance": {"title": "Musical Accompanist - Modern Dance", "description": "Musical Accompanist - Modern Dance Apply now Work type: Temporary Staff Pool Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Fine & Performing Arts Position Title Musical Accompanist - Modern Dance About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of Fine and Performing Arts supports Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. About the Position Under minimal direction and in cooperation with various dance faculty, candidate will provide piano and/or percussion accompaniment and musical guidance during dance technique, modern and improvisation classes. This is a temporary position. Required Qualifications Improvise and perform music with appropriate rhythms and tempos by observing the movement of dancers. Prepare and be consistent and the studio Ability to play piano or percussion instruments proficiently and follow musical direction. Ability to read musical notation. Ability to maintain cooperative working relationships with students, faculty and staff. Preferred Qualifications Knowledge of methods and techniques of dance accompaniment Ability to learn ballet and contemporary terminology Experience playing piano and/or percussion for dance classes Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary $23.80 - $27.38/hour depending on qualifications and experience Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Administrative Professional Position . Please note, this position is currently not eligible for benefits. Application Instructions Application materials should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Closing Date Notes Position is open until filled. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 502905 Advertised: Sep 16 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503174/assistantassociate-professor-of-ethnic-studies-native-indigenous-studies": {"title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Ethnic Studies (Native & Indigenous Studies)", "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Ethnic Studies (Native & Indigenous Studies) Apply now Work type: TT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Humanities & Social Science Position Title Assistant/Associate Professor of Ethnic Studies (Native & Indigenous Studies) About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of Humanities and Social Sciences and the Department of Ethnic Studies support Western's mission, which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. The Ethnic Studies Department at Western Washington University centers the histories, epistemologies and lived experiences of minoritized peoples and Indigenous Nations. Our approach is intersectional and transnational while highlighting the unique perspectives, contributions, and knowledges of Black, Indigenous, People of Color, and other minoritized communities. Our courses challenge systems of power and oppression, and advance liberation, emancipation, and self-determining future. For more information about the department, see the website at https://chss.wwu.edu/ ethnic - studies . About the Position The Department of Ethnic Studies at Western Washington University is inviting applications for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor in Native and Indigenous Studies, beginning Fall 2027. We seek a community-engaged interdisciplinary candidate who combines scholarly work on pressing Indigenous issues with a demonstrated record of deep commitments to Native American communities. We are especially interested in scholars whose teaching, research, and scholarship engage one or more of the following: Native sovereignty, food sovereignty, activism, environmental justice, Native ecologies, tribal governance, cultural revitalization, alternative epistemologies. We are open to all disciplines. The successful applicant will be well qualified to teach introductory and advanced undergraduate courses in Native and Indigenous Studies and Ethnic Studies and will participate in the collaborative building of our new department. Additional responsibilities include standard quarter-system teaching assignment (2-2-2); exemplary teaching; active program of scholarly activity; service to the department, college, university, and profession through a range of activities. The successful candidate will be expected to fulfill teaching and mentoring responsibilities in ways that provide equitable and inclusive learning environments for all students and will offer a strong record of community-engaged scholarship and research ethics. Required Qualifications PhD at time of application in American Indian, Native American, Indigenous Studies, Ethnic Studies, or other relevant field in the Humanities and/or Social Sciences. A demonstrated ability to teach introductory and advanced theory and methods courses (undergraduate). A demonstrated research record that leads to publication in peer-reviewed journals and/or other forms of scholarly dissemination. A demonstrated ability to work effectively with diverse students and cultivate learning environments that are equitable and inclusive of students with diverse social identities and backgrounds. Ability to work effectively with diverse colleagues, including a demonstrated ability and commitment to healthy communication and collaborative work practices that are culturally-responsive and appropriate. For appointment at the Associate Professor level (tenure track), demonstrated significant research and evidence of successful teaching commensurate with standards for promotion to Associate Professor in the Department, College, and University. Preferred Qualifications A commitment to building a strong Ethnic Studies Department through curriculum development as well as demonstrated leadership. Additional expertise in the Pacific Northwest. Demonstrated commitment to collaborative and community-engaged research. Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary The anticipated starting salary range for assistant professor appointment, tenure-track is generally between $71,500 \u2013 $74,500, with placement within the position\u2019s salary range being based on qualifications and professional experience. The entire salary range for the assistant professor position is $71,500 - $86,366, with the upper end of the salary range typically being achieved through collectively bargained salary adjustments. The starting salary range for associate professor appointment, tenure-track is generally between $80,000-$85,000. The entire salary range for the associate professor position is $80,000-$98,538. In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the entire salary ranges provided above reflect the anticipated full salary range in the position at the given rank, with progression in ranks occurring as outlined in the University\u2019s tenure and promotion process, per the collective bargaining agreement. Relocation assistance may be available per University guidelines and dependent on available budget. Salary and Start-up funds are to be determined upon being offered the position. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions To be considered for the position, application materials must be submitted via WWU\u2019s PageUp, Western's online employment management system. Candidates should upload the following as part of a complete application: 1) a letter of application specifically addressing the qualifications; 2) a CV; 3) a separate statement addressing demonstrated ability and commitment to cultivating learning and working environments that are equitable and inclusive of students, faculty, and staff with diverse social identities and backgrounds; and evidencing commitment to fulfill teaching and mentoring responsibilities in ways that provide equitable and inclusive learning environments for all students; 4) evidence of successful teaching (e.g., philosophy on teaching, student evaluations, course materials, etc.); 5) sample publications (no more than two) 6) graduate transcripts; and 7) the names and contact information of three professional references. Do not send letters of recommendation; three letters of recommendation will be required at the semi-finalist stage and these will be requested of candidates' referees via the PageUp system. Finalists may be asked for student course evaluations if they were not provided with the application. For questions regarding the position, application process, or department, contact the Department Manager at ethnic.studies@wwu.edu. Closing Date Notes Application review begins October 26, 2026; position is open until filled. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503174 Advertised: Sep 11 2026 08:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503211/psychology-nontenure-track-faculty-vacancy-pool": {"title": "Psychology - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool", "description": "Psychology - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool Apply now Work type: NTT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Humanities & Social Science Position Title Psychology - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of Humanities and Social Sciences and the Psychology Department support Western\u2019s mission, which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. All are encouraged to apply, including women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. The Department of Psychology has 35 permanent full-time faculty. In addition to our undergraduate B.S. psychology major, the Psychology department offers undergraduate degrees in Behavioral Neuroscience and master\u2019s programs in Experimental Psychology, School Counseling, and Clinical Mental Health Counseling. The department promotes five primary areas of teaching and research expertise (Social, Clinical, Developmental, Cognitive, and Neuroscience), but cross-area interests and collaborations are encouraged, supported, and flourishing. We are also home to the internationally recognized Center for Cross-Cultural Research . About the Position The Psychology Department maintains a vacancy pool of qualified persons interested in temporary teaching. In anticipation of possible openings throughout the academic year, applications are accepted continuously for temporary non-tenure track teaching positions on a part-time basis with occasional opportunities for temporary, full-time appointments. Positions may become available at any time and are typically filled on a quarterly basis. Position Responsibilities: The department, college and University value teaching, and we expect you to be an excellent teacher; to be current in your discipline; to engage students actively in their own learning including discussion, writing, and analysis; and to set high standards regarding course content. You are expected to provide students with feedback on their coursework and examinations, and to be available to students through regularly scheduled office hours in keeping with department policy. You must have your students evaluate your course(s) using the standard University evaluation forms, or others approved by the provost, and you must make those evaluations available to the department chairs and faculty for annual review. Required Qualifications Doctorate or ABD in Psychology or closely related discipline Successful teaching experience in higher education Demonstrated ability and commitment to cultivating learning environments that are equitable and inclusive of students with diverse social identities and backgrounds Preferred Qualifications Doctorate in Psychology or closely related discipline Evidence of scholarship Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary $1,324 - $1,613 per credit, commensurate with experience and qualifications Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions A cover letter and curriculum vitae are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. You will be asked to provide contact information of three references. Required Supplemental Materials Sample syllabi and teaching evaluations from relevant courses. Closing Date Notes Applications are accepted continuously for potential NTT faculty openings; please apply at your earliest convenience. Vacancy pool closes no later than September 30, 2027. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503211 Advertised: Sep 08 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Sep 30 2027 23:55 Pacific Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503221/music-nontenure-track-faculty-vacancy-pool": {"title": "Music -  Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool", "description": "Music - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool Apply now Work type: NTT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Fine & Performing Arts Position Title Music - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool About the University Western Washington University, with over 14,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 98,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of Fine and Performing Arts and the Music Department support Western's mission, which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. All are encouraged to apply, including women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. The Department of Music advances artistry, creativity, pedagogy, and scholarship as modeled and guided by faculty and realized by the undergraduate and graduate population. We are a collaborative community where the love of music is acted upon with vibrancy and unity. The Department of Music serves approximately 280 undergraduate majors and 12 graduate students annually, and offers BA, BMus and MMus degrees in multiple concentration areas, as well as minors in Music and Jazz Studies. The Department is comprised of twenty full-time faculty and approximately thirty-six part-time faculty. The department serves as a focal point for the campus and the regional community through collaboration, participation and leadership in music performance and instruction. Visit the department\u2019s website for more information. About the Position The Music Department maintains a vacancy pool of qualified persons interested in possible openings throughout the academic year and summer sessions for temporary, limited term teaching positions on a part-time basis, with occasional opportunities for temporary, full-time appointments. Positions may become available at any time and are typically filled on a quarterly basis. Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree in music or equivalent professional experience Demonstrated excellent communication skills and professionalism Demonstrated commitment to cultivating learning environments that are equitable and inclusive of students with diverse social identities and backgrounds Preferred Qualifications MM, PhD, DMA Previous teaching experience Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Commensurate with experience and qualifications Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions A cover letter and curriculum vitae are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. You will be asked to provide contact information of three references. This vacancy pool is refreshed annually; please see applicable close date information below. Applicants who continue to remain interested in being considered for on-going opportunities should re-submit updated materials to the new pool, once posted. Closing Date Notes Applications are accepted continuously for potential NTT faculty openings; please apply at your earliest convenience. Vacancy pool closes August 31, 2027. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503221 Advertised: Sep 02 2026 08:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Aug 31 2027 23:55 Pacific Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503183/henry-g-schwarz-visiting-lecturer-in-mongolian-studies": {"title": "Henry G. Schwarz Visiting Lecturer in Mongolian Studies", "description": "Henry G. Schwarz Visiting Lecturer in Mongolian Studies Apply now Work type: NTT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: Academic Affairs Position Title Henry G. Schwarz Visiting Lecturer in Mongolian Studies (Winter Quarter 2028) About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Departments The East Asian Studies Program and the Office of Global Engagement support Western's mission, which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. All are encouraged to apply, including women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. About the Position East Asian Studies and the Office of Global Engagement are seeking qualified candidates for the winter academic quarter 2028 (December 16, 2027 - March 15, 2028). The successful candidate will be responsible for teaching one 5-credit course in any academic discipline, with a focus on the study of Mongolia; plus an additional 3 hours per week involved in relevant professional service related to the Western Libraries Mongolia Collection and promotion of Mongolian Studies in the campus and local communities. (0.45 FTE appointment) The Visiting Lecturer will be expected to be current in their discipline, to engage students actively in their own learning, and to maintain high standards regarding course content. They will provide students with feedback on their coursework and examinations and to be available to students through regularly scheduled office hours in a designated location. The Visiting Lecturer will have students evaluate their course using the standard University evaluation forms and to make those evaluations available to the department. Required Qualifications All-but-Dissertation (or academic equivalent) at the time of application in any discipline, with a teaching focus on the study of Mongolia Demonstrated experience teaching at the college or university level Demonstrated experience teaching students from different social identities and backgrounds Preferred Qualifications Ph.D. (or academic equivalent) at the time of application in any discipline, with an active teaching and research focus on the study of Mongolia Experience living in Mongolia Fluency in Mongolian Experience with teaching methods that incorporate regular and meaningful opportunities for students to participate in their own learning Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary This is a part-time position (0.45 FTE) for one academic quarter. Total compensation for the 5-credit course is $15,000, plus up to a $2,500 travel allowance. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions This position is not benefits eligible. Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions A cover letter and curriculum vitae are required and should clearly address 1) your experience related to the position responsibilities; and 2) the required and preferred qualifications. In the application, provide contact information of three references. Inquiries about the position may be submitted to: Dr. Mark Greenberg , Director of Faculty Global Engagement. Closing Date Notes Application review will begin November 6, 2026 and remain open until filled. Finalists will be interviewed via video conferencing. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503183 Advertised: Sep 01 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503160/roofer": {"title": "Roofer", "description": "Roofer Apply now Work type: Permanent Full Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: Business and Financial Affairs Position Title Roofer About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department Facilities Development and Operations supports Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. About the Position This is the journey-level classification within the roofing series, responsible for performing skilled roofing work related to the construction, maintenance, repair, and renovation of institutional facilities. Duties include operating a wide range of roofing tools and equipment, including but not limited to torches, rollers, pressure washers, ladders, lifts, and other hand tools associated with the roofing trade. The position requires a self-motivated individual capable of working in various weather conditions and performing assigned duties independently with minimal or no direct supervision while consistently maintaining high standards of safety, workmanship, quality, and productivity. Duties/Responsibilities include: Perform preventive maintenance on roofing systems, including inspections, gutter cleaning, roof sweeping, drain cleaning and repair, roof patching, emergency repairs, and parapet maintenance. Install and repair a variety of roofing materials and systems, including insulation, built-up roofing, metal roofing, tile roofing, TPO, EPDM, flashing, drain basins, spray coatings, and related components. Remove and dispose of existing roofing materials, including flashing, insulation, aggregate, composition roofing, drain basins, metal roofing, and tile. Inspect, service, maintain, and repair roofing tools and equipment. Prepare material and labor estimates and submit accurate roofing material lists to supervisors. Apply caulking and sealants as needed using industry best practices. Maintain inventory of roofing materials and load and unload supplies and equipment. Clean and maintain roofs, job sites, and roofing equipment. Apply below-grade waterproofing products. Identify needed repairs and recommend corrective actions. Update roofing maintenance and service records as required. Set up and operate specialized equipment, including aerial work platforms (AWPs), and safely use hand and power tools. Provide technical information and support to estimators, engineers, designers, and architects. Follow safe work practices, including fall protection, fall arrest, Lockout/Tagout procedures, and all applicable safety regulations. Assist in maintaining and updating fall protection plans. Drive service vehicles and obtain a commercial driver's license when required. Perform physically demanding work, including standing, walking, lifting, carrying, bending, kneeling, climbing, reaching, and accessing confined spaces, with or without reasonable accommodation. Lead, instruct, and assist others as needed. Perform work in related trades as assigned. Work primarily outdoors, often at heights using ladders, lifts, scaffolding, and other access equipment, while complying with all safety and PPE requirements. Safely use a full-body harness and other required fall protection equipment. Communicate daily with supervisor and colleagues using a two-way, hand-held radio. May be required to operate a computer, printer, fax machine, photocopier, digital camera, and other data-processing equipment. Required Qualifications High school diploma or equivalent. Completion of a recognized apprenticeship, journey-level status in a roofing union, or four years of applicable work experience in a commercial or industrial environment. Mechanically proficient with the ability to work independently in all aspects of the trade. Valid driver\u2019s license or the ability to obtain one prior to employment. Skilled in journey-level roofing product application and the use of specialized trade equipment. Basic knowledge of carpentry and sheet metal work as related to roofing. Ability to communicate effectively, both verbally and in writing. Ability to read and understand written information and instructions. Strong interpersonal skills with a positive, friendly, and cooperative approach when working with students, faculty, staff, and the public. Ability to read blueprints, plans, and sketches and estimate materials and time required for project completion. Strong time management skills and the ability to work under pressure while meeting schedules and deadlines. Ability to work safely alone while recognizing when additional assistance is needed and notifying a supervisor in advance. Ability to safely use and maintain hand and power tools required for the trade. Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated experience with built-up roofing (torch-down), bituminous, 3-tab, TPO, EPDM, Hypalon, and metal roofing systems. Good working knowledge of related trades, including carpentry, welding, sheet metal, insulation, and painting, gained in a commercial or industrial environment. Strong knowledge of applicable Washington Administrative Code (WAC) regulations and local building codes. Strong knowledge of Washington Industrial Safety and Health Act (WISHA) and Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) rules and regulations, standard job safety practices, and the proper use of a respirator. Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary $4,538 - $5,264/month depending on qualifications and experience Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Classified Positions Bargaining Unit This position is part of bargaining unit B and is covered by the collective bargaining agreement with Washington Federation of State Employees. Application Instructions Application materials should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Closing Date Notes Position is open until filled Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503160 Advertised: Aug 27 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503204/critical-disability-studies-nontenure-track-faculty-vacancy-pool": {"title": "Critical Disability Studies \u2013 Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool", "description": "Critical Disability Studies \u2013 Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool Apply now Work type: NTT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: Academic Affairs Position Title Critical Disability Studies \u2013 Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. Western Washington University\u2019s Bellingham Campus sits upon the unceded ancestral lands of the Lummi Nation and Nooksack Tribe. We affirm that indigenous communities are still here and still provide leadership in our shared communities. About the Department The Institute for Critical Disability Studies (ICDS) was established in 2022. It is a cross-disciplinary initiative, drawing faculty from across the humanities, sciences, performing arts, and beyond. The ICDS offers a minor in Critical Disability Studies, which consists of three core courses and electives from across the university. We are committed to an ethos of disability justice, which means we are devoted to social justice and leadership of those most impacted by disability issues, meaning disabled, d/Deaf, queer, and BIPOC people themselves. All are encouraged to apply, including women, trans and queer people, people of color, disabled people, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. About the Position The Critical Disability Studies program maintains a vacancy pool of qualified persons interested in teaching with the program. In anticipation of possible openings throughout the academic year and summer sessions, applications are accepted continuously for temporary part-time non-tenure track teaching positions with occasional opportunities for temporary, full-time appointments. Positions may become available at any time and are typically filled on a quarterly basis. The position is responsible for teaching a subset of courses in the Critical Disability Studies program, which currently consists of DISA 201 (Stories of Disability in the World), DISA 330 (Critical Disability Studies), DISA 350 (Topics in Critical Disability Studies), and DISA 450 (Capstone in Critical Disability Studies). Other experimental courses and electives may be added in the future. All courses require expertise in the field of critical disability studies, and in the case of DISA 350, expertise in a disability-related topic. The holder of this position will be responsible for: Teaching one or more DISA classes, bearing between 3 and 5 credits (typically 4 or 5 credits) Utilizing online teaching technologies, including Canvas LMS Holding at least one regularly scheduled weekly office hour per DISA course (holding variable or on-demand office hours is optional) The position is open to face-to-face teaching or teaching in remote online, or hybrid formats. Hybrid or remote work must be in accordance with the University's Policy on Managing Telework Arrangements , which limits remote work to individuals who are residents of Washington state or Canada upon job start. The ICDS values teaching, and we expect you to be an excellent teacher; to be current in your discipline; to engage students actively in their own learning including discussion, writing, and analysis; and to set high standards regarding course content. You are expected to provide students with feedback on their coursework and examinations, and to be available to students through regularly scheduled office hours in keeping with department policy. You must have your students evaluate your course(s) using the standard University evaluation forms, or others approved by the provost, and you must make those evaluations available to the ICDS co-directors for annual review. Required Qualifications Master\u2019s Degree or higher in fields related to critical disability studies, accessibility, or disability culture and identity; and/or expert knowledge in a special topics area Demonstrated potential as a teacher Knowledge of critical disability studies and/or lived experience with disability culture Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with diverse students, faculty, and staff, including a commitment to cultivating learning environments that are equitable and inclusive of students with diverse social identities and backgrounds Preferred Qualifications Experience teaching critical disability studies courses at the college level Experience developing critical disability studies coursework and curriculum at the college level Specialty in a discipline-specific topic related to disability (for instance disability applied to dance or disability applied to literature) Scholarly research and publication in the area of critical disability studies Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary $1,200-$2,200 per Credit, Commensurate with experience and qualifications Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions A cover letter and curriculum vitae are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Also submit two additional documents: a 1-page statement explaining your commitments to Access, Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion in your teaching, and a brief description of how you would approach the courses you intend to teach, including sample readings and assignments. You will be asked to provide contact information of three references. They will receive an automatically-generated request to provide a reference letter as soon as your application is submitted through the PageUp hiring portal. Applicants who have prior college teaching experience should submit evidence of effective teaching practices as supplemental information, one or more of the following: course syllabi, student course evaluations, and/or peer observation letters. Closing Date Notes While this pool is listed as \"Open Continuously\" please note this does not indicate there is a current opening. Applications are accepted continuously for potential NTT faculty openings; please apply at your earliest convenience. Applicant materials are reviewed on an as-needed basis, and you will only be contacted if an opportunity is available. This vacancy pool is refreshed annually; please see applicable close date information below. Applicants who continue to remain interested in being considered for on-going opportunities should re-submit updated materials to the new pool, once posted. Vacancy pool closes no later than August 31, 2027. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Western Washington University, with over 16,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western is the highest-ranking public, master's-granting university in the Pacific Northwest, according to the 2022 U.S. News & World Report rankings. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. Western Washington University\u2019s Bellingham Campus sits upon the unceded ancestral lands of the Lummi Nation and Nooksack Tribe. We affirm that indigenous communities are still here and still provide leadership in our shared communities. Job no: 503204 Advertised: Aug 26 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Aug 31 2027 23:55 Pacific Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503187/executive-director-office-of-equity-civil-rights-title-ix-compliance": {"title": "Executive Director, Office of Equity, Civil Rights & Title IX Compliance", "description": "Executive Director, Office of Equity, Civil Rights & Title IX Compliance Apply now Work type: Permanent Full Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: President, Equity and Inclusion Position Title Executive Director, Office of Equity, Civil Rights & Title IX Compliance About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The Office of Equity and the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance support Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. About the Position Provide strategic direction and operational oversight for the Office of Equity and the Title IX and Civil Rights Compliance unit. The Executive Director fosters a diverse, equitable, inclusive, and accessible learning and working environment while ensuring institutional compliance with civil rights mandates. The Executive Director will also lead the Civil Rights and Title IX office and is responsible for overseeing compliance with civil rights and equal employment opportunity laws, regulations and policies related to race, ethnicity, color, creed, religion, national origin, citizenship or immigration status, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, gender identity and expression, age, genetic status, marital status, disability, veteran or military status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. The Office of Equity is the institutional point of contact for federal and state agencies for civil rights and equal opportunity compliance inquiries and compliance confirmation and is responsible for responding to related external agency complaints and investigations. Position Duties and Responsibilities 60% - Administer and oversee the University\u2019s Equal Opportunity policies and procedures in all aspects of employment and education, ensuring compliance across the University with federal and state civil rights and equal employment opportunity laws and regulations, including Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), and the Washington Law Against Discrimination. Manage the Office of Equity and serve as institutional subject matter expert for civil rights and Title IX complaints, including interpretation of laws and agency guidance. Provide oversight and direction for investigations into alleged discrimination and harassment, and allegations of sexual misconduct. Lead responses to external agency complaints, including EEOC, OCR, Dept. of Education, and Washington State agency complaints. Research, implement, and evaluate activities, procedures, and practices which support the university in achieving equitable and inclusive recruitment, hiring, and employment practices. Collaborate with Human Resource and academic departments to establish best practices within the University. Implement and ensure compliance requirements are met for all Title IX and anti-discrimination training for university students, staff and faculty. Develop and facilitate requested climate assessment tools for university stakeholders. 15% - Serve as the University's Title IX Coordinator Administer Title IX guidance and grievance procedures to the university community in a trauma-informed way and coordinate provision of resources and support for individuals who experience sexual harassment, sexual assault, relationship violence, and sex discrimination. Lead the work of deputy Title IX Coordinators as needed. 15% - Supervise University\u2019s ADA Coordinator(s). Oversee the institution\u2019s ADA program, serving as the lead ADA Coordinator and/or supervising the ADA and Deputy coordinator(s) as needed. Oversee policies, procedures, and responses to complaints. Provide technical guidance to the university about obligations under the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and grievance procedures for individuals who experience discrimination on the basis of disability. Mediate and resolve disputes that arise as a result of the implementation of ADA accommodations. 10% - Lead CRTC Office Oversee office personnel and budget. Maintain office climate of inclusion, integrity, impartiality, and care for staff and clients. Represent the unit and/or the University on committees, task forces, and other local and state bodies. Required Qualifications JD and a minimum of (8) years of progressive experience addressing the legal, education, administrative and/or management responsibilities of a university, or an equivalent combination of education and professional work experience. Administrative experience in higher education. Advanced knowledge and experience interpreting and applying civil rights, equal employment opportunity, and Title IX laws and regulations. Experience leading trauma-informed and culturally appropriate investigations Knowledge of higher education policies and practices and experience in promoting principles and practices of diversity, equity, and inclusion. Understanding of, sensitivity to, and respect for the diverse academic, socio-economic, racial, ethnic, religious, and cultural backgrounds, disabilities, gender identities, and sexual orientations of students, faculty, and staff. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, including demonstrated ability to build trusting working relationships with people from a wide range of constituencies in a diverse university community to ensure successful outcomes. Strong presentation, training, and facilitation skills with the ability to engage students, faculty, staff, and administrators with varying degrees of experience in examining and addressing issues of civil rights, equity, and justice. Excellent project and time management skills. Flexibility, adaptability, and capacity to work in a dynamic work environment. Demonstrated experience in effectively supervising assigned staff. Experience with alternative dispute resolutions and mediations Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated knowledge of/experience in Title IX regulations as they apply to NCAA rules, policies, and practices regarding gender equity in intercollegiate athletics. Experience working within collective bargaining environments and shared governance structures. Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Hiring range is $147,764 - $169,928/year depending on qualifications and experience. Through longevity position tops out at $192,094/year. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Administrative Professional Position . Application Instructions A cover letter and resume are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Please include the names and contact information of three professional references. References will only be contacted for finalists, with notice provided beforehand. Closing Date Notes Priority review begins October 5, 2026; position is open until filled Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503187 Advertised: Aug 25 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503196/geology-faculty-ntt-vacancy-pool": {"title": "Geology Faculty NTT Vacancy Pool", "description": "Geology Faculty NTT Vacancy Pool Apply now Work type: NTT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Science & Engineering Position Title Geology Faculty NTT Vacancy Pool About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of Science and Engineering and the Geology Department support Western's mission, which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. The Geology Department at WWU is committed to excellence in both teaching and research. Our goal is to offer the highest possible quality education in the geological sciences at the undergraduate and graduate levels. The mission of our department is to serve three main populations: graduate students, undergraduate geology majors, and undergraduates from other departments for their general education courses. For all of these students we strive to create excitement about discovery and the process of geologic inquiry. We want to develop in all students an appreciation of how geological processes affect the earth and society so that they will be environmentally responsible, scientifically literate citizens. We strive to produce majors with an interdisciplinary content background in geology and the physical sciences who are competent in the field, who can work collaboratively, conduct original research, and effectively communicate their results. For more information please visit our website: Welcome to the Geology Department! | Geology Department | Western Washington University (wwu.edu) About the Position This position will be filled as needed to teach a variety of possible courses offered by the WWU Geology Department. These courses may range from 100-level Introduction to Geology to upper-division (300-400) or Graduate level courses in the candidate's specialty that the department may have a need for in a given term. Required Qualifications A MS degree in the Geosciences or an allied field is required. A demonstrated commitment to quality, student-centered teaching practices, the ability to teach and work in an environment with a diverse student, faculty, and staff is also required. For specific needs, especially at the upper division, an appropriate combination of graduate-level coursework and experience in the field of geoscience that is needed is also required. Preferred Qualifications Relevant college level teaching experience is preferred. A PhD in the Geosciences or an allied field is also preferred. Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Commensurate with experience and qualifications; salary for this position is $1100 to $2500 per course credit, and will vary widely depending on the number of courses needed and according to the experience and qualifications of the applicants. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions A cover letter and curriculum vitae are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. You will be asked to provide contact information of three references. Application Instructions Please review the Geology Department's course offerings in the current WWU catalog to better understand our courses, their content, and our different degree programs. Because the temporary teaching needs of the Geology Department will vary from year to year, please contact the Geology Department Chair for questions about possible specific opportunities. The application pool will be refreshed annually during the summer prior to the start of the academic year, so candidates are encouraged to review their applications and reapply each year as they choose. Closing Date Notes Applications are accepted continuously for potential NTT faculty openings; please apply at your earliest convenience. Vacancy pool closes no later than August 15, 2027. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503196 Advertised: Aug 25 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Aug 15 2027 23:55 Pacific Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503124/budget-analyst": {"title": "Budget Analyst", "description": "Budget Analyst Apply now Work type: Permanent Full Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: Business and Financial Affairs Position Title Budget Analyst About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department Strategy, Management & Budget supports Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. Strategy, Management & Budget is the university's home for budget planning, management, analysis, and financial resource stewardship. The department operates under the Vice President for Business & Financial Affairs and works closely with various campus units to deliver budgetary and financial planning services. The team is dedicated to enhancing financial accountability and transparency throughout the university from building the university operating budget to communicating the university\u2019s financial positions such as the final expenditures and outcomes. About the Position Under the direction of the Director for Budget and Financial Planning, the Budget Analyst is responsible for providing analyses and reports related to planning, developing, implementing, and administrating Western\u2019s operating budget. This position may include supporting development of the state biennial and supplemental budget requests; leading the preparation of fiscal notes during legislative session; managing the University\u2019s institutional budget; and providing other policy, fiscal and operational analysis in support of the university operating budget. Position Duties and Responsibilities 45% - Legislative Session & Budgetary Support Assist with biennial and supplemental budget development, coordinate decision packages, enter and verify budgetary data in the state Office of Financial Management (OFM) systems. Prepare and submit the biennial and annual allotment expenditure plans for all allotted operating funds; prepare monthly journal vouchers to report expenditure actuals to the state; and monitor actuals on a monthly basis to ensure internal allocations and allotments remain reconciled. Analyze proposed & adopted state operating budgets and identify potential impacts to WWU. Review pending legislative bills; assist with preparation and submission of fiscal notes and legislative information requests; collaborate with subject matter experts to identify fiscal impacts and cost estimates. Coordinate meetings with other Washington public universities, WWU Governmental Relations, and OFM during legislative session to ensure alignment across the sector in our approach to fiscal notes and budget development. 45% - Ongoing Budget Office Responsibilities Develop and facilitate Fall and Spring Management Reporting process by preparing resources, leading trainings, and assisting as a subject matter expert in support of unit completion of revenue, budget, and expense forecasting. Assist the Director and Assistant Vice President (AVP) in managing the university's institutional budget, including maintaining institutional memberships, implementing cash and budgetary transfers, ensuring timely invoice payments, tracking and reconciling institutional budget commitments, and ad hoc financial analysis to inform decision makers. Provide ad hoc financial analysis and expense forecasting to inform presentation materials and budget change recommendations related to university budget and financial information. Facilitate meetings with stakeholders to review current processes and practices, including communicating and translating technical information across various stakeholder groups. Conduct research of best practices among institutions of higher education for budget development, maintenance, and reporting processes, assessing feasibility of adoption. Update, consolidate, and improve existing process manuals and internal Budget and Financial Planning publications. Serve on work teams as a subject matter expert. 10% - Other Duties as Assigned Serve as backup for several key Budget and Financial Planning roles; Assist senior leadership with ad hoc projects as requested. Conduct budget transfers, review position requests, help maintain the Budget and Financial Planning website, and fulfill other duties as assigned. Required Qualifications Bachelors degree in business or public administration, finance, or a field relevant to position or equivalent experience/education At least three years of professional experience related to budgets and financial management Demonstrated ability to translate complex fiscal information into clear, easy to understand language Demonstrated excellent interpersonal, verbal communication, analytical, critical thinking, and creative problem-solving skills. Ability to work independently and manage competing deadlines on multiple projects. Demonstrated experience working with people of diverse backgrounds as a member of an inclusive team. Ability to handle sensitive financial and business information in a confidential manner. Preferred Qualifications Master\u2019s degree in business or public administration, finance, or a field relevant to position or equivalent experience/education Relevant work experience in higher education field Demonstrated experience working with financial or business system databases, especially Banner/Ellucian and Millennium systems Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Hiring range is $86,395 - $99,354/year depending on qualifications and experience. Through longevity position tops out at $112,314. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Administrative Professional Position . Application Instructions A cover letter and resume are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Please include the names and contact information of three professional references. References will only be contacted if necessary, with notice provided beforehand. Closing Date Notes Application review begins September 23, 2026; position is open until filled. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503124 Advertised: Aug 24 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503168/marine-and-coastal-science-program-nontenure-track-instructor": {"title": "Marine and Coastal Science Program - Non-Tenure Track Instructor", "description": "Marine and Coastal Science Program - Non-Tenure Track Instructor Apply now Work type: NTT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: Academic Affairs Position Title Marine and Coastal Science Program - Non-Tenure Track Instructor About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The Marine and Coastal Science Program (MACS) is an interdisciplinary major at WWU created to help students develop into confident, thoughtful, ethical scientists who are ready to address the growing challenges affecting our marine and coastal environments. The major combines resources and expertise from the Biology Department, the Chemistry Department, the Geology Department, the Department of Environmental Sciences, and the Shannon Point Marine Center, located in Anacortes about an hour away from main campus. The major is built around a cohort model in which students engage deeply with their peer group while learning about the tools, concepts, and context of marine science. MACS welcomes one cohort of 30 students per year, for a total of 60-90 students in the program at any given time. There are eight core faculty members in the program, numerous affiliate faculty, four additional staff members, and an academic director. The Marine and Coastal Science Program supports Western's mission, which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. About the Position The Marine and Coastal Science Program maintains a vacancy pool of qualified persons interested in temporary teaching positions. In anticipation of possible openings throughout the academic year and summer session, applications are accepted continuously for temporary, non-tenure track teaching positions on a part-time basis with occasional opportunities for temporary, full-term appointments. Positions may become available at any time and are typically filled on a quarterly basis. This is a non-tenure track position at the rank of Instructor for the duration of each assigned courses and/or labs specified. There is no expectation of continued employment. The Program and the University value teaching and the candidate is expected to be an exemplary teacher; to be current in the discipline; to engage students actively in their own learning, including discussion, writing, and analysis; and to provide students with feedback on their course work and examinations; and to be available to students through regularly scheduled office hours. The instructor is required to have the students evaluate the course(s) using the standard University evaluation forms and to make those evaluations available to the MACS director, academic director and faculty for annual review. Required Qualifications Masters or PhD in related field, or commensurate experience Experience teaching university-level courses Demonstrated ability and commitment to cultivating learning environments that are equitable and inclusive of students with diverse social identities and backgrounds Preferred Qualifications PhD in related field Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Commensurate with experience and qualifications Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions A cover letter and curriculum vitae are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. You will be asked to provide contact information of three references. Closing Date Notes Applications are accepted continuously for potential NTT faculty openings; please apply at your earliest convenience. Vacancy pool closes no later than August 31, 2027. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503168 Advertised: Aug 21 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Aug 31 2027 23:55 Pacific Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503122/endowed-professorship-of-jewish-history": {"title": "Endowed Professorship of Jewish History", "description": "Endowed Professorship of Jewish History Apply now Work type: TT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Humanities & Social Science Position Title Endowed Professorship of Jewish History About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The Department of History at Western Washington University consists of eighteen tenured or tenure-track faculty whose courses serve a very active history major and make significant contributions to the university\u2019s general education curriculum. History courses cover nearly every historical era and much of the globe. The department encourages its faculty to participate in interdisciplinary programs such as the Ray Wolpow Institute for the Study of the Holocaust, Genocide, and Crimes Against Humanity, Canadian-American Studies, East Asian Studies, Latin American Studies, Women, Gender, and Sexuality Studies, and the Honors program. Faculty in the department provide high-quality courses that prepare majors and non-majors with skills in research, analysis, and writing. The department also houses a small but strong M.A. program in history. The history department supports Western\u2019s identity as an institution that welcomes and embraces diversity and encourages applications from diverse candidates. The Department of History is in the College of Humanities and Social Sciences. To learn more about the History Department, visit https://chss.wwu.edu/history . About the Position The position includes teaching 6 in-person courses on the quarter system, including two lower-division surveys, History of the Holocaust, and three other courses in the candidate's specialty. Candidates are also expected to pursue research and publication in their area of interest. Required Qualifications PhD or ABD at time of application in History or related field. If ABD, all degree requirements must be completed by June 15 th of the first year of employment (e.g., June 15, 2028). Specialization in Jewish history, with the ability to teach courses in Jewish history and the Holocaust A demonstrated ability to work effectively with diverse students and cultivate learning environments that are equitable and inclusive of students with diverse social identities and backgrounds. Preferred Qualifications Ph.D preferred Demonstrated potential for effective teaching and high-quality scholarship Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary The starting salary range for assistant professor appointment, tenure-track is generally between $73,000-$78,500, with placement within the position\u2019s salary range being based on qualifications and professional experience. The entire salary range for the assistant professor position is $73,000-$91,000, with the upper end of the salary range typically being achieved through collectively bargained salary adjustments. In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the entire salary range provided above reflects the anticipated full salary range in the position at the given rank, with progression in ranks occurring as outlined in the University\u2019s tenure and promotion process, per the collective bargaining agreement. Relocation assistance maybe available per university guidelines. Salary and start-up funds are to be determined upon being offered the position. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions A letter of application and curriculum vitae are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. As part of your application, you will be asked to provide contact information for three references. Please do not upload letters of recommendation; these will only be requested for semi-finalists, and the PageUp application system will send out requests for three letters of recommendation to the references you provide. Required Supplemental Materials Graduate transcript, a chapter- or article-length writing sample, sample syllabi for an introductory course in Jewish history as well as an upper-level course in Jewish history or the Holocaust. Closing Date Notes Application review begins November 1, 2026; position is open until filled. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503122 Advertised: Aug 18 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503172/physicsastronomy-nontenure-track-faculty-vacancy-pool": {"title": "Physics/Astronomy - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool", "description": "Physics/Astronomy - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool Apply now Work type: NTT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Science & Engineering Position Title Physics/Astronomy - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of Science and Engineering and the Department of Physics & Astronomy support Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. About the Position The Department of Physics & Astronomy maintains a vacancy pool of qualified persons interested in temporary teaching positions. In anticipation of possible openings throughout the academic year and summer sessions, applications are accepted continuously for temporary non-tenure track teaching positions on a part-time basis with occasional opportunities for temporary, full-time appointments. Positions may become available at any time and are typically filled on a quarterly basis. Position Responsibilities: Lecture and/or lab instruction in Physics and/or Astronomy courses Required Qualifications M.S. in Physics, Astronomy or related field, or at least five years of experience with college-level physics instruction Ability to work effectively with diverse students, faculty and staff, and commitment to cultivating learning environments that are equitable and inclusive of students with diverse social identities and backgrounds Commitment to evidence-based, student-centered teaching methods Preferred Qualifications PhD in Physics, Astronomy or related field Demonstrated successful teaching experience Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary $1,175 - $1,577 per credit hour, commensurate with experience and qualifications Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions A letter of application addressing the above qualifications, teaching experience, a curriculum vitae, and contact information of three references are required. In your cover letter, please specifically address your ability to work effectively with diverse students, faculty and staff, as well as commitment to cultivating learning environments that are equitable and inclusive of students with diverse social identities and backgrounds. Closing Date Notes Applications are accepted continuously for potential NTT faculty openings; please apply at your earliest convenience. Vacancy pool closes no later than August 31, 2027. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503172 Advertised: Aug 10 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Aug 31 2027 23:55 Pacific Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503042/accommodation-support-coordinator": {"title": "Accommodation Support Coordinator", "description": "Accommodation Support Coordinator Apply now Work type: Permanent Full Time Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: Academic Affairs Position Title Accommodation Support Coordinator About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The Disability Access Center supports Western's mission , which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. About the Position Under general direction, this position coordinates the day-to-day operations of accommodation implementation services for students registered with the Disability Access Center. The role focuses on organizing and tracking logistics for a variety of accommodations (such as notetaking support, alternative furniture, remote participation, alternative testing, and future services as program needs evolve) so that support is delivered accurately, on time, and in line with university and accommodation policies. The position maintains records and tracking tools, collaborates with campus partners, and supports student staff and front-desk operations as part of a broader accommodation services team. This is a strong early-career role for someone interested in disability access, student services, or higher education administration. The StateHR classification for this position is Program Coordinator. This is a full-time, 10-month cyclic position. Required Qualifications High school graduation or equivalent; and Two years of experience in a relevant program area such as student services, disability/access services, higher education administration, or a closely related field that includes coordination of activities or services or An equivalent combination of education and experience that provides the required program coordination skills and knowledge Proficiency in Microsoft Office 365 (specifically Excel for data tracking and Outlook for complex scheduling and within case management systems Demonstrated ability to communicate complex, step-by-step procedural information clearly and empathetically to diverse stakeholders (students, faculty, and staff) both in person and via email Experience working in a fast-paced environment where priorities shift based on immediate student or program needs Preferred Qualifications Bachelor\u2019s degree in Social Sciences, Education, Business Administration, or a related field (or an equivalent combination of education and experience). Foundational understanding of the Social Model of Disability and the difference between \"access\" and \"success.\" Familiarity with common assistive technologies (e.g., Kurzweil, Dragon, JAWS, or Glean) and the basics of Document Remediation (OCR). Familiarity with university structures, specifically how a Disability Services Office interacts with the Registrar, Facilities, Contracts, or IT. Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary $3,955 - $5,291/month depending on qualifications and experience Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Classified Positions Bargaining Unit This position is part of bargaining unit A and is covered by the collective bargaining agreement with Washington Federation of State Employees. Application Instructions Application materials should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. Closing Date Notes Application review begins August 11, 2026; position is open until filled Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503042 Advertised: Jul 28 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503065/assistant-professor-of-theatre": {"title": "Assistant Professor of Theatre", "description": "Assistant Professor of Theatre Apply now Work type: TT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Fine & Performing Arts Position Title Assistant Professor of Theatre About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of Fine and Performing Arts and the Department of Theatre and Dance support Western's mission, which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. The College of Fine and Performing Arts comprises the departments of Art/Art History, Design, Music, and Theatre and Dance, housed in eight buildings with five performance spaces, including a Performing Arts Center overlooking Bellingham Bay. The College also is home to the Music Library and Western Gallery and Public Art Collection, one of the top ten outdoor sculpture collections in the country. About the Position The Department of Theatre and Dance invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Theatre beginning September 16, 2027. We are seeking a dynamic, collaborative, energetic, creative, and empathetic artist-teacher who wants to be part of an undergraduate-focused theatre department which combines the strengths of a liberal arts program with the rigor of professional practice. The successful candidate will share in our department and university\u2019s strong commitment to cultivate inclusive and equitable learning and creative environments. The responsibilities of this position include teaching introductory and advanced courses in theatre and performance, primarily Introduction to Theatre, Directing, and Theatre Education, and may include other courses to support our curriculum. This person will be directing productions in our department\u2019s mainstage season, and will be a principal contributor to our vibrant theatre department, collaborating with fellow faculty, mentoring students, and making a significant positive contribution to the life of our department. The teaching load is between 27 and 30 credits spread over three quarters in the academic year. The successful candidate will have a demonstrated record of activity in the practice of theatre and/or theatre scholarship. They will be passionate about working with undergraduate students in a liberal arts environment and will be expected to fulfill recruiting, advising, teaching, and mentoring responsibilities that provide equitable and inclusive learning environments for all students. They will participate in service activities for the department, college, and university, and will maintain an ongoing practice of creative and/or scholarly activity in the discipline. Required Qualifications One of the following: MFA in Acting, Performance, Directing, Playwrighting or closely related field at time of application; or PhD in Theatre Arts or closely related field at time of application Demonstrated ability to cultivate learning and working environments that are equitable and inclusive of students and employees with diverse cultural backgrounds and social identities Evidence of successful teaching experience in higher education, and/or commensurate professional experience Record of creative activity and/or scholarship related to theatre arts Demonstrated effective skills in collaboration and communication Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated experience in and enthusiasm for teaching Directing, Theatre Education, and a large Introduction to Theatre survey course to majors and non-majors Additional demonstrated expertise in an area of the candidate\u2019s interest related to theatre. Evidence of successful experience in directing Evidence of successful creative activity in professional venues The ability to work and collaborate across disciplines: outside the arts, across campus, and in the community Experience in curriculum development A dedication to a liberal arts educational model compatible with the mission of WWU Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary The starting salary range for assistant professor appointment, tenure-track is generally between $69,000-$74,000, with placement within the position\u2019s salary range being based on qualifications and professional experience. The entire salary range for the assistant professor position is $69,000-$86,000, with the upper end of the salary range typically being achieved through collectively bargained salary adjustments. In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the entire salary range provided above reflects the anticipated full salary range in the position at the given rank, with progression in ranks occurring as outlined in the University\u2019s tenure and promotion process, per the collective bargaining agreement. Relocation assistance may be available per University guidelines. Salary and start-up funds are to be determined upon being offered the position. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions Application materials must be submitted via WWU\u2019s PageUp system and include the following: Cover letter addressing how you meet the required and preferred qualifications Curriculum vitae Statement of teaching philosophy Accessibility, diversity, equity, and inclusion statement Submit a one-page document discussing your ability and commitment to work effectively with underrepresented students and to foster and promote an environment of accessibility, diversity, equity, and inclusion; you may draw upon your personal as well as professional experience in developing this statement. For more information about Western\u2019s commitment to this movement, please see Western President\u2019s page on Advancing Inclusion and Diversity. Portfolio of your creative and/or scholarly accomplishments (this may consist of a document containing a hyperlink or digital materials). Names and contact information for three professional references. References will only be contacted for finalist candidates. Inquiries may be directed to the Theatre and Dance Search Committee Chair, Prof. Evan Mueller, at Evan.Mueller@wwu.edu . Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire. Employment-based visa sponsorship may be considered depending on the candidate's current immigration status; however, the University is unable to sponsor new H1B petitions or cover associated filing or processing fees at this time. Closing Date Notes Application review begins September 16, 2026. Position closes November 10, 2026 and applications will no longer be accepted after that date. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503065 Advertised: Jun 22 2026 08:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Nov 10 2026 23:55 Pacific Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503003/industrial-and-systems-engineering-in-the-peninsulas-nontenure-track-faculty-vacancy-pool": {"title": "Industrial and Systems Engineering in the Peninsulas - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool", "description": "Industrial and Systems Engineering in the Peninsulas - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool Apply now Work type: NTT Faculty Location: Poulsbo, WA Categories: College of Science & Engineering Position Title Industrial and Systems Engineering in the Peninsulas - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool About the University Western Washington University is a public institution that was founded in 1893 and is comprised of about 15,000 students. It is recognized as the top public masters-granting university in the Pacific Northwest by US News & World Report. The Chronicle of Higher Education names Western as one of the best colleges in the nation to work for, winning honors in two categories: teaching environment, and tenure clarity and process. Western Washington University\u2019s presence on the Olympic and Kitsap Peninsulas includes academic centers in Bremerton, Poulsbo, and Port Angeles, developed in partnership with Olympic College and Peninsula College. These sites expand access to bachelor\u2019s degrees for place-bound students and support regional workforce needs. Current programs include Business Administration (Poulsbo), Cybersecurity (Poulsbo), Environmental Science and Environmental Studies (Port Angeles and Poulsbo), Natural Resource Management (Port Angeles and Poulsbo), Multidisciplinary Studies (all three sites), and Early Childhood and Elementary/Special Education (Bremerton). About the College The College of Science and Engineering (CSE) consists of eight academic departments: Biology, Chemistry, Computer Science, Electrical and Computer Engineering, Engineering and Design, Geology, Mathematics, and Physics and Astronomy. In addition to the academic departments, the college is home to Western\u2019s Science, Mathematics, and Technology Education (SMATE) program. Western has extensively invested in new facilities, instructional innovation, and the recruitment of world-class faculty for the sciences in recent years. Thus, students in the college are engaged in high-quality classroom, laboratory, and hands-on research experiences that foster deep foundational disciplinary knowledge as well as the tools to engage in the creation of new scientific knowledge. The overall mission of CSE is to promote lifelong learning through state-of-the-art programs that emphasize experiential learning and scholarship, to produce globally impactful discoveries, innovation, and student-engaged research, to provide equitable and inclusive pathways in STEM for students to achieve their career goals in the State of Washington and beyond, and to engage with the broader community by building and sustaining strong regional academic, industrial, and other community partnerships. About the Department The Engineering & Design Department offers two ABET-accredited undergraduate degrees in Manufacturing Engineering (MFGE) and Materials Science & Engineering (MSE), along with a NASAD-accredited degree in Industrial Design. The Industrial and Systems Engineering (ISE) program is newly established and is expected to welcome its first cohort in Fall 2026. In addition, Western Washington University offers an ABET-accredited degree in Electrical & Computer Engineering (EECE) through the recently established EECE Department. Guided by its mission, the Engineering & Design Department is dedicated to excellence in teaching and scholarship. Its programs emphasize creative problem-solving, analytical skill development, and experiential learning, preparing graduates to be industry-ready engineers and designers who can contribute immediately to regional and global innovation. About the Position The Department of Engineering & Design at Western Washington University invites applications for a non-tenure-track faculty position in support of the Industrial & Systems Engineering Program at Western on the Peninsulas. This is a new 2+2 Bachelor of Science degree in Industrial & Systems Engineering on the Peninsulas (ISE) designed to serve students on the Olympic Peninsula and the greater region. The first two years of ISE program are completed through the Associate of Science - Track 2 (AS-T2) program at Olympic College or other regional AS-T2 granting institutions, followed by the final two years through WWU. ISE prepares graduates for careers with the Puget Sound Naval Shipyard, Amazon, logistics and supply chain organizations, healthcare systems, and other regional employers. The curriculum emphasizes systems design, data analytics, operations research, human factors, quality and continuous improvements, and project management, with strong integration of applied learning, hybrid course delivery, and industry-sponsored capstone projects. The program is being developed with ABET accreditation as a central goal, ensuring rigorous academic preparation and industry recognition. The Engineering & Design Department maintains a vacancy pool of qualified persons interested in temporary teaching in ISE program. In anticipation of possible openings throughout the academic year and summer sessions, applications are accepted continuously for temporary, non-tenure track teaching positions on a part-time basis with occasional opportunities for temporary, full-time appointments. Positions may become available at any time and are typically filled on a quarterly basis. Position Responsibilities: Teach Industrial & Systems Engineering courses as directed by the ISE Program Faculty Lead and the Department Chair. The department, college and University value teaching, and we expect you to be an excellent teacher; to be current in your discipline; to engage students actively in their own learning including discussion, writing, and analysis; and to set high standards regarding course content. You are expected to provide students with feedback on their coursework and examinations, to be available to students through regularly scheduled office hours in keeping with department policy, and to collect assessment data on student learning and participate in efforts for accreditation related to your teaching assignment. You must have your students evaluate your course(s) using the standard University evaluation forms, or others approved by the provost, and you must make those evaluations available to the department chairs and faculty Lead for annual review. Required Qualifications B.S. degree in Industrial & Systems Engineering or a related discipline Demonstrated potential for effective teaching Demonstrated commitment to fulfilling working, teaching, and mentoring responsibilities in ways that provide equitable and inclusive environments for all students, faculty, and staff with diverse social identities and backgrounds Preferred Qualifications Recent, relevant industry experience M.S. or Ph.D. in in Industrial & Systems Engineering or related discipline Successful teaching experience Conditions of Employment Must be able to travel and spend time at multiple locations, including peninsula\u2019s locations (Bremerton and Poulsbo). All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Commensurate with experience and qualifications Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions Applicants are required to submit a cover letter and curriculum vitae that clearly address their experience in relation to the position responsibilities, as well as the required and preferred qualifications. When applicable, submission of prior student evaluations of teaching is strongly encouraged. Applicants will also be asked to provide contact information for three professional references. This vacancy pool is refreshed annually. Please refer to the applicable closing date information below. Applicants who wish to remain under consideration for future opportunities are encouraged to resubmit updated application materials to the new pool once it is posted. Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire. Employment-based visa sponsorship may be considered depending on the candidate's current immigration status; however, the University is unable to sponsor new H1B petitions or cover associated filing or processing fees at this time. Closing Date Notes Applications are accepted continuously for potential NTT faculty openings; please apply at your earliest convenience. Vacancy pool closes no later than December 31, 2026. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503003 Advertised: Jun 05 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Dec 31 2026 23:55 Pacific Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503018/assistant-or-associate-professor-of-marketing": {"title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Marketing", "description": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Marketing Apply now Work type: TT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Business and Economics Position Title Assistant or Associate Professor of Marketing About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of Business and Economics and the Marketing Department support Western's mission, which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. The Department of Marketing has 200 undergraduate majors. Our faculty are active, collaborative researchers who enthusiastically support student success through in-person classes and advising. The College and Department advance Western\u2019s mission by fostering an inclusive, student-centered environment and are committed to serving an increasingly diverse student body. For more information about the College of Business and Economics and the Department of Marketing, visit https://cbe.wwu.edu and https://cbe.wwu.edu/fmkt/marketing-program . About the Position A full-time, tenure track position in Marketing at the Assistant or Associate Professor level is available, with an anticipated start date of September 2027. Responsibilities include teaching courses like Market Research, Consumer Behavior, Principles of Marketing and advanced electives at the undergraduate or MBA levels. Scholarship expectations include publishing in high-tier marketing journals. Additional responsibilities include contributing to student and program success through service commensurate with rank. Required Qualifications Ph.D. or ABD at time of application in Marketing, or in Business/Management with a concentration in Marketing, from an AACSB-accredited institution. If ABD, all degree requirements must be completed by June 15th of the first year of employment (e.g., June 15, 2028). Evidence of, or clear potential for, publication in top-tier, peer-reviewed marketing journals. Demonstrated excellence in teaching, or strong evidence of teaching effectiveness, at the college level Ability to effectively teach and engage an increasingly diverse student population. Strong communication, professionalism, and organizational skills. For appointment at the Associate Professor level (tenure track), demonstrated significant research and evidence of successful teaching commensurate with standards for promotion to Associate Professor in the Department, College, and University. Preferred Qualifications Professional experience in the marketing field. Experience publishing in top-tier, peer-reviewed marketing journals. Experience creating a student-centered learning environment. Ability to collaborate effectively with colleagues and contribute to a supportive, inclusive academic community aligned with the department mission of inclusive excellence in marketing education and Western Washington University\u2019s values . Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary The starting salary range for assistant professor appointment, tenure-track is generally between $130,000-$135,000, with placement within the position\u2019s salary range being based on qualifications and professional experience. The entire salary range for the assistant professor position is $130,000-$157,000, with the upper end of the salary range typically being achieved through collectively bargained salary adjustments. The starting salary range for associate professor appointment, tenure-track is generally between $143,000-$148,000. The entire salary range for the associate professor position is $143,000-$172,000. In accordance with RCW 49.58.110 , the entire salary ranges provided above reflect the anticipated full salary range in the position at the given rank, with progression in ranks occurring as outlined in the University\u2019s tenure and promotion process, per the collective bargaining agreement. Relocation assistance may be available per University guidelines. Salary and Start-up funds are to be determined upon being offered the position. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions To be considered for the position, application materials must be submitted via WWU\u2019s Electronic Application System. Please log in at https://hr.wwu.edu/careers-faculty , and use the online application system. Please address any questions about the position to Dr. Catherine A. Armstrong Soule, Professor of Marketing, at cat.armstrongsoule@wwu.edu or 360-650-4685. Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire. Employment-based visa sponsorship may be considered depending on the candidate's current immigration status; however, the University is unable to sponsor new H1B petitions or cover associated filing or processing fees at this time. Required Supplemental Materials Interested candidates must submit the following documents: Complete an application A cover letter that addresses all the required qualifications Curriculum Vitae Research statement that clearly articulates your scholarly agenda, highlighting your potential to consistently publish impactful work in high-tier marketing journals while demonstrating the practical relevance of your findings to the field Teaching statement, including evidence of your commitment to a student-centered approach, specifically how you engage diverse learners in an effective, inclusive classroom environment As part of your application, you will be asked to provide contact information for three references. Please do not upload letters of recommendation; the PageUp application system will send out requests for three letters of recommendation to the references you provide. Finalists will be asked to provide copies of transcripts, evidence of scholarship, and teaching evaluations (if available). Closing Date Notes The review of applications will begin on June 30, 2026. The position is open until filled. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503018 Advertised: Jun 03 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503050/urban-and-environmental-planning-policy-ntt-vacancy-pool": {"title": "Urban and Environmental Planning & Policy NTT Vacancy Pool", "description": "Urban and Environmental Planning & Policy NTT Vacancy Pool Apply now Work type: NTT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of the Environment Position Title Urban and Environmental Planning & Policy NTT Vacancy Pool About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of the Environment and the Urban and Environmental Planning & Policy Department support Western's mission, which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds, and those with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. About the Position Undergraduate faculty in the UEPP Department, with programs in urban planning/design, sustainability, studio courses, and environmental/natural resource policy. Our programs emphasize engaged pedagogies and critical perspectives. We encourage and collaboratively work within the nexus of urban/natural/environmental planning and policy. Please refer to the UEPP courses listed in the WWU catalog . Required Qualifications Masters or doctorate in a related field from an accredited institution. Under exceptional circumstances, a candidate with a BA or BS and a minimum of 5 years of experience can be considered. Evidence of community designs, models, exhibitions, community service learning, and other applied works. Demonstrated abilities in planning and policy history, methods, applications, and theory and related planning and policy fields. Demonstrated continued learning of current and advanced knowledge and practices in planning and policy fields. Demonstrated continued engagement in related discipline and/or professional activity. Demonstrated ability to cultivate environments that are equitable and inclusive of students, faculty, and staff with diverse social identities and backgrounds and ability and commitment to promoting diversity and inclusion within the department. Record of supporting the success of students or professionals traditionally underrepresented in the field. Preferred Qualifications Teaching, research, and/or service within the planning and public policy professions. Skill in experiential and critical pedagogies suitable to classroom and/or field settings. Ability to manage field practicum logistics and planning, including with community partner organizations or other entities. Successful experience using an online learning management system, such as Canvas. Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Starting salary of $1,385 per credit, commensurate with experience and qualifications. Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions The following materials are required: Current curriculum vitae A letter of application addressing the above qualifications, outlining teaching philosophy, and demonstrating student outcomes as applicable. UEPP is committed to diversity and inclusive excellence in our faculty, students, and staff, and we especially encourage candidates who share and can contribute to this commitment. Please provide a separate statement that addresses past and/or potential contributions to accessibility, diversity, equity, and inclusion. Names and contact information for three references* Verification of highest degree (unofficial transcript is acceptable and may be scanned for electronic application and email purposes) If applying for a teaching position, please also include the following: List of current UEPP courses that the applicant feels qualified to teach as well as potential new courses Sample of complete syllabi of courses related to the applicant\u2019s field * Following the submission of your application, reference letters will be automatically requested from these individuals via the PageUp system. Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time of hire. Employment-based visa sponsorship may be considered depending on the candidate's current immigration status; however, the University is unable to sponsor new H1B petitions or cover associated filing or processing fees at this time. Closing Date Notes Applications are accepted continuously for potential NTT Faculty openings; please apply at your earliest convenience. Vacancy pool closes no later than May 30, 2027. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503050 Advertised: May 12 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: May 30 2027 23:55 Pacific Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503046/sociology-for-professionals-instructor-nontenure-track-faculty-vacancy-pool": {"title": "Sociology for Professionals Instructor - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool", "description": "Sociology for Professionals Instructor - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool Apply now Work type: NTT Faculty Location: Poulsbo, WA Categories: College of Humanities & Social Science Position Title Sociology for Professionals Instructor - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The city lies 90 miles north of Seattle and 60 miles south of Vancouver, British Columbia. Western has additional sites in Anacortes, Bremerton, Everett, Port Angeles, and Poulsbo. Western is recognized nationally for its successes, such as being named one of the top public master's-granting institutions in the Pacific Northwest for 25 years in a row by U.S. News & World Report. Western Washington University is committed to achieving excellence through advancing inclusive success, increasing our Washington impact, and focusing on transformational education grounded in the liberal arts and sciences and based on innovative scholarship, research, and creative activity. Western's greatest strength is the outstanding students, faculty, staff, and alumni/ae who make up its community. Western supports an inclusive governance structure for all and provides a learning and working environment in which everyone can thrive. In pursuit of this excellence, individual employees are expected to establish and maintain productive and effective inclusive working relationships amongst diverse populations including staff, faculty, administration, student, and external constituents. Further, individual employees are expected to have the ability to operationalize sustainability concepts (economic, societal, environmental) into all aspects of performing their job duties. About the Department The College of Humanities and Social Sciences and the Department of Sociology support Western's mission, which states that together with our students, staff, and faculty, we are committed to making a positive impact in the state and the world with a shared focus on academic excellence and inclusive achievement. We encourage applications from women, people of color, people with disabilities, veterans, and other candidates from underrepresented backgrounds and with diverse experiences interested in this opportunity. The Department of Sociology is comprised of thirteen permanent full-time faculty engaged in a wide range of research activities who often involve undergraduate students in research and teaching. The Sociology Department is one of sixteen departments in the College of Humanities and Social Sciences (CHSS). For more information, please visit the Department website . Sociology at WWU on the Peninsulas program equips students with essential skills and knowledge to advance their careers. The curriculum consists of courses in professional development, program evaluation, and applied sociological theory, preparing students for real-world challenges. With a focus on critical thinking, writing proficiency, and statistical analysis, graduates are prepared for careers in social work, law, education, and criminal justice, where they can address complex societal issues with expertise and insight. Sociology for Professionals , Western on the Peninsula website. About the Position The Sociology Department maintains a vacancy pool of qualified persons interested in temporary teaching for Western on the Peninsula, Sociology for Professionals at the Olympic College locations in the Bremerton and Poulsbo, WA region. In anticipation of possible openings throughout the academic year and summer sessions, applications are accepted continuously for temporary non-tenure track teaching positions on a part-time basis with occasional opportunities for temporary, full-time appointments. Positions may become available at any time and are typically filled on a quarterly basis. Position Responsibilities: The department, college and University value teaching, and we expect you to be an excellent teacher; to be current in your discipline; to engage students actively in their own learning including discussion, writing, and analysis; and to set high standards regarding course content. You are expected to provide students with feedback on their coursework and examinations, and to be available to students through regularly scheduled office hours in keeping with department policy. You must have your students evaluate your course(s) using the standard University evaluation forms, or others approved by the provost, and you must make those evaluations available to the department chairs and faculty for annual review. Required Qualifications MA in Sociology or closely related social science field Demonstrated potential for excellent teaching in sociology Demonstrated ability and commitment to cultivating learning environments that are equitable and inclusive of students with diverse social identities and backgrounds Preferred Qualifications Ph.D. or ABD in Sociology or closely related discipline Successful prior teaching experience Strong interpersonal communication skills Evidence of scholarship Conditions of Employment All employees must comply with our Immunization policies, including Proof of Rubeola Measles Immunity within 60-days of hire. Please reach out to HR@wwu.edu if you need information regarding medical or religious exemption and applicable accommodations. Salary Commensurate with experience and qualifications Benefits Information Benefits Overview for Faculty Positions Bargaining Unit United Faculty of Western Washington Application Instructions A cover letter and curriculum vitae are required and should address your experience related to the position responsibilities and the required and preferred qualifications. You will be asked to provide contact information of three references. For information about the department and potential openings, contact Seth Feinberg, Sociology Department Chair at 360-650-3864 or feinbes@wwu.edu . This vacancy pool is refreshed annually; please see applicable close date information below. Applicants who continue to remain interested in being considered for on-going opportunities should re-submit updated materials to the new pool, once posted. Closing Date Notes Applications are accepted continuously for potential NTT faculty openings; please apply at your earliest convenience. Vacancy pool closes 12/31/2026. Western Washington University (WWU) is an equal opportunity employer. In compliance with applicable laws, WWU does not discriminate on the basis of race, ethnicity, color, national origin, age, citizenship or immigration status, pregnancy, use of protective leave, genetic status, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, creed, religion, veteran or military status, disability or the use of a trained guide dog or service animal (including a service animal in training) by a person with a disability, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law, in its programs or activities, including employment, admissions, and educational programs. See WWU\u2019s Policies on Prohibiting Discrimination Based on A Protected Class and Prohibiting Discrimination Based on Sex . Inquiries may be directed to the Office of Civil Rights and Title IX Compliance, Title IX and ADA Coordinator, Western Washington University, Old Main 126 (MS 9021), 516 High Street, Bellingham, WA 98225; 360.650.3307 (voice) or 711 (Washington Relay); crtc@wwu.edu WWU is committed to providing reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. To request this document in an alternate format or to request an accommodation, please contact Human Resources Disability Services , 360.650.3751 or 711 (Washington Relay). Annual Security and Fire Safety Report: This report is provided pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (\"The Clery Act\"). It includes statistics for the previous three calendar years concerning reported crimes that occurred on Western's campus; in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Western; and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from, the campus. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security, such as policies concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. You can obtain a copy of this report in printed or alternate formats by contacting the Office of Student Life at student.life@wwu.edu . The report can be found at: Annual Security and Fire Safety Report . All new employees must comply with the immunization policy and show employment eligibility verification as required by the U.S. Citizen and Immigration Service before beginning work at WWU. A thorough background check will be conducted on all new hires and rehires, which includes a sexual misconduct background check . Job no: 503046 Advertised: May 08 2026 09:00 Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Dec 31 2026 23:55 Pacific Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"}, "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/793/cw/en-us/job/503037/sociology-instructor-nontenure-track-faculty-vacancy-pool": {"title": "Sociology Instructor - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool", "description": "Sociology Instructor - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool Apply now Work type: NTT Faculty Location: Bellingham, WA Categories: College of Humanities & Social Science Position Title Sociology Instructor - Non-Tenure Track Faculty Vacancy Pool About the University Western Washington University, with over 15,000 students in seven colleges and the graduate school, is nationally recognized for its educational programs, students and faculty. The campus is located in Bellingham, Washington, a coastal community of 90,000 overlooking Bellingham Bay, the San Juan Islands and the North Cascades Mountain range. The 